// Generated by tools/build-help.mjs from the website's docs-content.js. Do not edit: edit the site's articles and run the builder again.
window.MOHelpData = {"site":"https://lukagrcar.com/","docs":"https://lukagrcar.com/apps/meshoptimiser/docs.html","groups":[{"id":"start","title":"Start here","blurb":"What the app is, what it needs, and how to get from a download to your first model on screen."},{"id":"around","title":"Find your way around","blurb":"The window, the mouse, selecting, hiding, the parts tree, search, undo, tabs and settings."},{"id":"lighter","title":"Make it lighter","blurb":"The tools that remove triangles and parts, one at a time, and a sensible order to use them in."},{"id":"look","title":"Look and arrange","blurb":"View modes, materials, moving and measuring, standing a model on the floor, and adding parts."},{"id":"save","title":"Save and export","blurb":"Keep your work as a scene, or write a file for another program."},{"id":"reference","title":"Reference","blurb":"Every shortcut, every command-line option, the file formats and what is in the folder."},{"id":"help","title":"Help","blurb":"What to do when something goes wrong, and answers to common questions."}],"articles":[{"slug":"what-it-is","group":"start","title":"What MeshOptimiser is","summary":"A free app that runs on your own computer. It turns a heavy CAD file into a light 3D file and lets you inspect, clean up and export it.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser is a free, open-source app that runs on your own computer. You give it a heavy 3D file from an engineering program. It gives you back a much lighter file that a web page or another 3D program can load quickly, and a viewer to look at the model, clean it up and export it."],["p","You do not need to know anything about 3D to start. These pages explain each word the first time it is used, and [Words you will meet](#glossary) lists them all in one place."],["h","The problem it solves"],["p","Engineers design machines in CAD programs (CAD stands for computer-aided design). They usually hand the design over as a STEP file, a common exchange format for CAD. A STEP file describes every part exactly, down to the last screw, so it is often very large and slow to show."],["p","A real assembly (a model made of many parts) can hold 400 identical bolts and 80 identical brackets. MeshOptimiser notices the parts that are the same and stores each shape once, with a list of the places where it sits. It also turns the exact CAD surfaces into triangles, which is what screens draw, and can leave out parts that are too small to see."],["terms",[["400 bolts × 50 KB","Become 1 shape of 50 KB and 400 positions."],["80 brackets × 12 KB","Become 1 shape of 12 KB and 80 positions."],["A 320 MB STEP file","Becomes an 11 MB compressed GLB. This is the example from the app’s own README, not a promise for every file."]]],["h","The two halves"],["list",["The converter. A small Python program, `step2glb.py`, reads a STEP file and writes a GLB file. GLB is a compact 3D format that browsers and most 3D programs can open.","The viewer. A page that opens in a window of its own and shows the model. Here you look around, select parts, remove what you do not need, reduce triangles, recolour, and export."]],["p","You do not have to run the two halves yourself. One launcher starts everything, and you drop a file on the window."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-viewer.webp","A rear derailleur in 43 parts and 1.1 million triangles: the parts tree on the left, the scene’s totals on the right."],["h","What you can do with it"],["list",["Open STEP, GLB, glTF, FBX, OBJ, 3MF and STL files.","See how heavy a model is, and which parts make it heavy.","Delete parts nobody will see, close holes, simplify parts and reduce triangles.","Rename, group and tidy the list of parts.","Recolour materials, move parts, measure distances and add standard parts from a library.","Save your work, and export to GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL, PLY, or a parts list."]],["h","What it is not"],["list",["It is not a CAD program. It reads STEP files but does not write them, and it does not edit exact CAD surfaces.","It is not an online service. Nothing is uploaded to the internet. See [Privacy and offline use](#privacy-offline).","It is not finished. The version is below 1.0, and things may change between versions."]],["note","MeshOptimiser runs on Windows and macOS, is free, and is published under the MIT licence."],["see",["glossary","requirements","first-start"]]]},{"slug":"glossary","group":"start","title":"Words you will meet","summary":"A plain-language glossary: STEP, mesh, triangle, GLB, instancing, decimation, WebGPU, virtual environment and the other terms these pages use.","blocks":[["p","You do not need to learn these before you start. Come back to this page when a word is new."],["h","Files and formats"],["terms",[["CAD","Computer-aided design. The programs engineers use to design real objects, such as SolidWorks or NX."],["STEP (.step, .stp)","A file format for exchanging CAD designs between programs. It stores exact surfaces, part names, colours and how the parts fit together."],["GLB","A compact 3D file made of triangles, with colours and part names inside one file. Browsers and most 3D programs can open it. This is the format MeshOptimiser writes by default."],["glTF","The same format as GLB, written as readable text. The app’s own format sheet describes it as the same capabilities, larger."],["FBX, OBJ, STL, PLY, USDZ, 3MF","Other 3D file formats. [File formats](#file-formats) says what each one is for."],["XCAF","The part of a STEP file that holds names, colours and the assembly tree. You see the word in the import settings."]]],["h","What a model is made of"],["terms",[["Part","One piece of the model: a bolt, a cover, a bracket. Each part has a row in the parts tree."],["Assembly","A model made of many parts, often arranged in groups inside groups."],["Group","A folder in the parts tree. It holds parts or other groups."],["Mesh","The surface of a part, built from many small flat triangles."],["Triangle (tri)","The smallest piece of a mesh. The app counts everything in triangles: more triangles mean a heavier model."],["Polygon","Another word for the flat pieces of a mesh. In this app it means the same as triangle."],["Vertex","A corner point of a triangle. The plural is vertices."],["Normal","A hidden arrow on the surface that tells the light which way the surface faces. Wrong normals make a part look oddly shaded."],["Bounding box (bbox)","The smallest box that a part fits inside. The app uses it to measure how big a part is."],["Origin and pivot","The origin is the point 0,0,0 of the scene. A pivot is the point a part turns around."],["Material","What a surface looks like: its colour and how shiny or metallic it is. Many parts can share one material."]]],["h","What the app does to a model"],["terms",[["Tessellation","Turning the exact curved surfaces of a CAD file into triangles. Finer tessellation means smoother curves and more triangles."],["Instancing","Storing one mesh for a shape that repeats, plus the list of places where it sits. 400 identical bolts become one bolt and 400 positions."],["Decimation","Removing triangles from a mesh while keeping its overall shape. It is lossy: detail that is removed does not come back, except through undo."],["Lossy","An action that throws detail away to save size."],["Compression","Packing the same data into a smaller file. Meshopt and Draco are two methods for 3D files."],["Meshopt","A compression method for GLB files. The converter applies it through a separate tool called gltfpack, when that tool is installed."],["Draco","Another compression method for GLB files. It is a switch in the Export window."],["Scene","Everything in one tab of the app: the parts, their materials, the camera and the undo history."],["BOM","Bill of materials: a parts list. The CSV export writes one."]]],["h","The screen"],["terms",[["Viewport","The large 3D view in the middle of the window."],["Parts tree","The list of parts and groups in the left sidebar."],["Gizmo","The coloured arrows, rings or handles that appear on a selected part, for moving, rotating or scaling it."],["Orbit, pan, zoom","Turning the view around the model, sliding it sideways, and moving closer or further away."],["Isolate","Showing only the selected parts and hiding everything else for a moment."],["Draw call","One instruction to the graphics card to draw something. Fewer draw calls usually mean a smoother view. The optimisation report counts them."]]],["h","The technical side"],["terms",[["Python","A programming language. The converter and the small local server are written in it, so Python has to be installed."],["Virtual environment (.venv)","A private folder of Python add-ons that belongs to this app only. The launcher creates it inside the app folder the first time, so nothing else on your computer is changed."],["Terminal","A window where you type commands. On Windows it is called Command Prompt or PowerShell, on macOS it is called Terminal."],["Local server","A small program on your own computer that hands the app’s pages to your browser. Its address is normally `http://localhost:4242`. Localhost means this computer."],["PATH","The list of folders where your computer looks for programs. Python has to be on it for the launcher to find it."],["WebGPU","The modern way for a browser to use the graphics card. The viewer uses it when the browser has it."],["WebGL2","An older way to do the same. The viewer uses it when WebGPU is not available."],["Installing the app (PWA)","Chrome and Edge can add a web page to your desktop and Start menu as if it were a program. A page that supports this is called a progressive web app, or PWA."]]],["see",["what-it-is","file-formats","read-the-numbers"]]]},{"slug":"requirements","group":"start","title":"What you need","summary":"Windows or macOS, Python 3.10 to 3.12, a recent browser and about 2 GB of free disk space. How to check and install Python.","blocks":[["terms",[["Computer","Windows or macOS."],["Python","Version 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12. Version 3.13 is not supported yet, because one of the add-ons the converter needs (cadquery-ocp) is not available for it."],["Browser","One with WebGPU: a recent Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. For the app to open in a window of its own, the launcher looks for Chrome, Edge or Brave."],["Disk space","About 2 GB free for the Python environment that is created on the first run."],["Internet","Needed once, on the first run, to download the Python add-ons. After that the app runs without a connection."]]],["h","Check whether you have Python"],["steps",["Open a terminal. On Windows, press the Windows key, type `cmd` and press [[Enter]]. On macOS, open the app called Terminal.","Type the command below and press [[Enter]]. On macOS, type `python3` instead of `python`.","Read the answer. `Python 3.12.7` means version 3.12 is installed, which is fine. A message that Python was not found means you need to install it."]],["code","python --version"],["note","You can skip this check. The launcher looks for Python itself and prints the version it found."],["h","Install Python on Windows"],["p","There are two ways. The first is the simplest."],["steps",["Start the app with `start.bat` (see [Start it the first time](#first-start)).","If no Python is found, the window says so and asks whether to install Python 3.12 automatically. Press [[Y]].","Wait while it installs. The launcher then carries on by itself."]],["p","Or install it yourself:"],["steps",["Go to `https://www.python.org/downloads/` and download Python 3.12.","Run the installer. On its first screen, tick “Add Python to PATH”. This is the step people miss.","Finish the installer, then close any terminal windows that were open, so they pick up the change."]],["h","Install Python on macOS"],["p","The macOS launcher uses the command `python3`. If it is missing and you have Homebrew (a tool for installing software on a Mac), the launcher installs Python through Homebrew. Without Homebrew, download Python from `https://www.python.org/downloads/` and run the launcher again. Make sure the version is 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12."],["h","Optional: gltfpack"],["p","The converter can shrink its GLB files further with Meshopt compression. It does this through a separate free tool called gltfpack, and only when gltfpack is installed and on your PATH. The app works without it. The files are simply larger."],["see",["download","first-start","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"download","group":"start","title":"Download and unzip","summary":"Get the zip, unpack it into a normal folder, and learn why there is no installer.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser comes as one zip file. There is no installer. Everything, including its Python environment, lives in the folder you unpack."],["steps",["Open the Download page of this site and press the download button. Your browser saves a zip file, usually in your Downloads folder.","Unpack the zip. On Windows, right-click it and choose the option to extract everything. On macOS, double-click it.","You now have a folder, named something like `MeshOptimiser-main`. Move it wherever you like, for example into your Documents folder.","Open the folder. You should see files called `start.bat`, `start.command`, `serve.py` and `index.html`, among others."]],["note","Unpack the zip before you start anything. Windows lets you look inside a zip without unpacking it, and the launcher does not work from there."],["h","Where to put the folder"],["list",["Somewhere you are allowed to write files. The app creates two folders inside its own: `.venv` for its Python environment and `inbox` for converted files.","On a drive with about 2 GB free."]],["p","To remove the app later, delete the folder. Nothing is installed anywhere else, apart from Python itself if you installed it for this."],["see",["first-start","files-in-folder","stop-restart-update"]]]},{"slug":"first-start","group":"start","title":"Start it the first time","summary":"Double-click the launcher for Windows or macOS. What the first run sets up, how long it takes, and what you see when it is ready.","blocks":[["p","The first start takes about three minutes, because the launcher downloads and sets up what the converter needs. Every start after that takes a second or two."],["h","On Windows"],["steps",["Open the app folder and double-click `start.bat`.","A black window with white text opens. This is the launcher. Leave it open.","It works through four numbered steps. `[1/4] Checking Python...` looks for Python and prints the version it found.","`[2/4] Checking local Python environment...` creates the folder `.venv` (about 30 seconds) and installs the add-ons (one to three minutes). You need an internet connection for this step.","`[3/4] Starting local server on http://localhost:4242` starts the small server that the app runs on.","The app opens in a window of its own and shows the start screen."]],["note","If the launcher finds no Python, it offers to install Python 3.12 for you. Press [[Y]] to accept. See [What you need](#requirements)."],["h","On macOS"],["steps",["Open the app folder and double-click `start.command`.","If macOS refuses with “Operation not permitted”, right-click `start.command` and choose “Open”. macOS blocks a double-click on a freshly unzipped script the first time.","A Terminal window opens. It creates the folder `.venv`, installs the add-ons, and prints `Starting server...`.","The app opens and shows the start screen."]],["h","What you see"],["p","The start screen has links to Help, the keyboard shortcuts and Settings at the top, and a row of tiles: {{Open file…}} (or drop a file anywhere on the screen), {{New scene}} to start with nothing and {{Import…}} to add a file to the scene that is already open. Under them are four shapes to start from (Cube, Sphere, Cylinder and Torus) and the list of recent files, with a search box above it and a {{Clear all}} button. The list is empty the first time."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-start.webp","The start screen: open or drop a file, reopen a recent one, or start from a shape."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["The launcher window is the server. If you close it, the app stops working. Keep it open while you work and see [Stop, restart and update](#stop-restart-update) for the tidy way to quit.","The app opens in a window with no address bar and no browser tabs when Chrome, Edge or Brave is installed. Without one of those, it opens as a tab in your usual browser. Both work the same.","The address is normally `http://localhost:4242`. If something else on your computer already uses that number, the launcher tries another one and prints the address it chose.","It uses your usual browser profile, so your settings and recent files are remembered between starts."]],["h","Start with a file"],["p","On Windows you can drag a `.step` or `.glb` file onto `start.bat` instead of double-clicking it. The app starts and opens that file. For a STEP file the launcher window first asks a few questions, such as the tessellation quality. Press [[Enter]] at each question to accept the suggested answer."],["see",["first-file","install-as-app","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"install-as-app","group":"start","title":"Install it as an app window","summary":"Since v0.13.0 the app opens in its own window. You can also install it for a desktop shortcut, and fold its title bar away.","blocks":[["p","From v0.13.0 the launcher already opens MeshOptimiser in a window of its own: a title bar and the app, with no address bar, no browser tabs and no extension buttons. It does this when Chrome, Edge or Brave is on your computer, and you do not have to install anything for it."],["p","Installing is an extra. It gives the app its own icon, a shortcut on the desktop and in the Start menu, and a window to itself."],["h","Install it"],["steps",["Start the app as usual, so the local server is running.","Open the app in an ordinary tab of Chrome or Edge. Type the address the launcher printed, normally `http://localhost:4242`, into the browser.","The browser offers to install the page as an app (“Install”). Accept it.","The app now has a shortcut and opens in its own window."]],["note","Installing does not replace the launcher. The installed app is a window onto the same local server, so start the server first with `start.bat` or `start.command`. Without it the window has nothing to show."],["h","Fold the title bar away"],["p","In the installed app, the title bar has a small `^` button. Press it and the title bar disappears. The window’s own buttons (minimise, maximise, close) float over the top right corner, and the app’s top bar becomes the title bar."],["list",["Drag an empty stretch of the top bar to move the window.","Buttons, tabs and fields in the top bar still work as before.","Press `^` again to bring the title bar back."]],["h","An ordinary browser tab instead"],["p","If you prefer the app in a normal tab, start the server from a terminal in the app folder with the option `--tab`. [The command-line converter](#command-line) explains how to open a terminal in the folder."],["code","python serve.py --tab"],["h","Start without the black window (Windows)"],["p","The file `start_hidden.vbs` starts the app the same way as `start.bat`, without showing the launcher window. Use `start.bat` when you want to see what the launcher is doing, for example when something does not start."],["see",["first-start","stop-restart-update","the-window"]]]},{"slug":"first-file","group":"start","title":"Open your first file","summary":"Drop a file on the window. What the import settings mean, what conversion does, how to follow its progress, and where the result is kept.","blocks":[["p","The app opens STEP (`.step`, `.stp`), GLB, glTF, FBX, OBJ, 3MF and STL files. A STEP file is converted first. The other formats open directly."],["h","Open a file"],["steps",["Drag the file from your file manager onto the app window. You can drop it on the start screen or on the 3D view. While you hold it over the view, the words {{Drop to open}} appear.","Or press [[Ctrl + O]] and pick the file. On the start screen the same button is called {{Open file…}}.","For a STEP file, a window called {{Import settings}} appears. The first time, leave everything as it is and press {{Import}}.","A progress card shows what is happening, with a running clock and a log of messages. A large assembly can take several minutes.","When it is done, the model appears in the 3D view. The parts tree on the left fills with its parts, and {{Properties}} on the right shows its totals."]],["note","Your original file is never changed. The app works on its own copy, and nothing is written to a file until you save or export."],["h","What conversion does"],["p","A STEP file holds exact surfaces. The converter, running in Python on your computer, turns it into a GLB file made of triangles. Along the way it:"],["list",["reads the names and colours of the parts and the tree they are arranged in;","tessellates the surfaces, which means it covers them with triangles;","finds parts that have the same shape and stores that shape once (instancing);","leaves out parts below a size you choose, if you ask for it;","compresses the result with Meshopt, if the tool gltfpack is installed."]],["h","The import settings for a STEP file"],["terms",[["Tessellation quality","How closely the triangles follow curved surfaces. The choices run from Coarse (1.0), the fastest with visibly faceted curves, to Ultra (0.05), slow and very smooth. Standard (0.5) is the recommended one."],["Colours & materials","Read colours, names and the hierarchy, or geometry only. Geometry only is the fastest and gives you neither colours nor a tree. It suits huge files where you only need the shape."],["XCAF read modes","Five switches for extra information in the file. Each one costs time. Turning off {{SHUO overrides}} saves the most on assemblies with many repeated parts."],["Drop parts smaller than (% of model)","Leaves out tiny parts during conversion. 0 keeps everything."],["Force re-convert","Ignores a stored conversion of the same file, if there is one, and converts from scratch."],["Collapse identical shapes into instances","Stores a repeated shape once. Leave it on."],["Append to current scene","Adds the file to the scene that is open, instead of opening it as a scene of its own."],["Fit view after loading","Moves the camera so the whole model is in view when it has loaded."],["Don't ask again for this format","Skips this window next time and uses the same answers. Leave it unticked until you know which settings you want."]]],["p","FBX, OBJ, 3MF and STL files get a shorter version of this window, with only the last three choices. GLB and glTF files open without it."],["h","Follow the progress, or stop it"],["list",["The progress card shows the latest message from the converter. {{Copy log}} copies all messages, which is useful when you report a problem.","{{Cancel}} stops the conversion.","If the conversion fails, the card says so. Press {{Copy log}} to keep the messages. See [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)."]],["h","Where the converted file goes"],["p","The converter writes the GLB into the folder `inbox`, inside the app folder. The copy of your STEP file that was made for the conversion is removed when it finishes. To keep a GLB somewhere you choose, with a name you choose, use [Save a scene](#save-scene) or [Export](#export)."],["h","More than one file"],["list",["Opening a second file does not replace the first. It opens in a new tab. See [Scene tabs](#scene-tabs).","To put two files into one scene, use {{Menu}}, then {{Import into scene…}}, or press [[Ctrl + Shift + O]].","To throw away every edit and load the file again as it was, use {{Menu}}, then {{Revert to source…}}."]],["see",["the-window","move-the-view","read-the-numbers","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"stop-restart-update","group":"start","title":"Stop, restart and update","summary":"Quit the app cleanly, start it again in a second or two, and move to a newer version without losing your settings.","blocks":[["h","Stop the app"],["steps",["Click {{Menu}} in the top bar and choose {{Quit app}}.","If the scene has parts in it, the app asks whether to save first. Choose to save, to quit without saving, or to cancel.","The page says that MeshOptimiser is closed and the server has stopped. On Windows the launcher window closes by itself.","Close the app window if it is still open."]],["p","Closing only the app window does not stop the server. The launcher window stays open and the server keeps running. You can also stop it there: click the launcher window and press [[Ctrl + C]], or close that window."],["h","Start it again"],["p","Double-click `start.bat` or `start.command` again. The launcher finds the `.venv` folder from the first run and reuses it, so the app is ready in a second or two."],["p","With the start screen’s resume option on, the file you had open last is offered at the top of the start screen. See [Settings](#settings)."],["h","Update to a new version"],["p","There is no updater. A new version is a new zip. This is a suggested way to move over, based on how the app stores things:"],["steps",["Quit the app.","Download the new zip and unpack it into a new folder, next to the old one.","If you want to keep converted files, copy them out of the old folder’s `inbox` folder.","Start the new version with its launcher. The first start sets up its Python environment again and takes about three minutes.","When you are happy with the new version, delete the old folder."]],["note","Your settings and the recent files list are kept by your browser, not in the app folder, so they are still there after an update."],["see",["first-start","files-in-folder","save-scene"]]]},{"slug":"the-window","group":"around","title":"The window","summary":"A tour of the screen: the top bar and tabs, the parts tree, the 3D view and its toolbars, the cards on the right, the drawers, the status bar and how to resize it all.","blocks":[["p","The window has four areas: a top bar, a left sidebar, the 3D view in the middle (called the viewport) and a right sidebar. A thin status bar runs under the viewport."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-viewer.webp","The whole window with an assembly open."],["p","When the app starts, a short splash screen shows the version and what the app is doing, then the window fades in. You can switch the splash off under {{Settings}}, {{General}}."],["h","Top bar"],["terms",[["The app’s mark","At the far left. Click it for the version number, what is new, links to the source code and to report a problem, {{Help & docs}} and the list of keyboard shortcuts."],["Menu","The file commands: {{New scene}}, {{Open file…}}, {{Import into scene…}}, {{Save scene…}}, {{Export…}}, {{Revert to source…}}, {{Scene settings…}}, {{Settings…}}, {{Help & docs}}, {{Welcome screen}} and {{Quit app}}."],["Sidebar button","Right after Menu. Shows or hides the left sidebar. When the sidebar is hidden, the button stays next to Menu."],["Scene tabs","One tab for each open scene, and a `+` to start a new one. See [Scene tabs](#scene-tabs)."],["Export","At the right. Writes the model to a file for another program. See [Export](#export)."],["Camera button","Next to Export. Saves a picture of the view. See [Screenshots](#screenshots)."]]],["h","Left sidebar"],["p","It shows the parts tree: every part of the model, arranged in groups, with a search field and three buttons above it ({{Collapse}}, {{Rename}}, {{Flatten}}). A {{Transform}} card with position, rotation and size can be shown at its foot. It floats over the bottom of the sidebar and takes its own room under the tree. The library of parts you can add is not in this sidebar any more. It is a drawer under the viewport (see below)."],["h","Viewport"],["terms",[["Tool strip, left edge","Move, rotate and scale tools, {{Dynamic place}} ([[D]]), a button for the transform card, then four view modes (solid, wireframe, X-ray, heatmap) and a button that highlights small parts. The Clay and CAD looks are in the {{Shading}} card on the right."],["View name, top centre","Says which camera view is active, {{Cam}} to begin with. Click it to switch to Top, Front or Side. Small pills to its right show a mode that is on: {{Isolated}}, {{Exploded}}, {{Measure}} and {{Snap}}. Click a pill to leave that mode."],["View cube, top right","A small cube that turns with the model. Click a face or an axis dot and the view turns there."],["Toolbar, bottom centre","Search, add a shape, {{Split}}, {{Fill holes}}, {{Smart fit}}, {{Select hidden parts}}, {{Fasteners}}, {{Measure}}, {{Library}}, {{Materials}}, and `…` for the list of all commands."],["Shortcut tips, bottom right","A faint list of what the mouse and keys do right now. It changes when you select something."],["Command cards, bottom left","A tool with settings, such as Fill holes, Align to floor or Dynamic place, opens as a card over the viewport. [[Enter]] runs it and [[Esc]] puts it away."]]],["h","Right sidebar"],["p","At the top is {{Properties}}. With nothing selected it describes the whole scene. With a selection it describes that: the name, the material, the triangle count with a bar that shows its share of the scene, and the number of vertices, the size and the mesh data. Under it are cards you open and close by clicking their title. A card slides open and shut. Every card starts folded except {{Properties}} and {{Shading}}, and the ones you open or close are remembered."],["terms",[["Shading","Every look for the Clay and CAD views as a small picture: Clay, Porcelain, Steel and Red wax, and the CAD looks Ceramic, Light and Mono. Click one to choose it and turn the view on. Click the lit one again to go back to Solid. The {{Outlines}} switch draws a fine dark line on the sharp edges of every part. See [Colours and materials](#colours-materials) and [View modes](#view-modes)."],["Selection & actions","Select all, invert, select similar, by colour, by name, and Merge, Group and Delete."],["Select similar","Sets how close a part has to look to the selected ones to count as similar."],["Inspect","{{Heavy parts}} (with a small count of how many there are), {{Filters & flags}}, {{Measurements}} (only while there is a measurement) and {{Exploded view}}."],["Clean up","{{Delete small parts}}, {{Auto cleanup}} and {{Tidy geometry}}."],["Reduce","{{Smart optimise}} and {{Reduce triangles}}, which holds Fit to budget, Decimate, Smart fit all parts and the optimisation report."]]],["p","Tools with their own settings, such as {{Align to floor}} and {{Find stacked copies}}, are not cards in the sidebar. They open over the viewport, from the toolbar, the list of all commands or the search."],["h","Status bar"],["p","Under the viewport: the number of vertices, the size of the mesh data, how many parts are selected (click it to frame them), how many are flagged (click it to isolate them), the frame rate, and a {{Console}} button. The numbers of parts and triangles are in {{Properties}}."],["h","Drawers"],["p","Three panels slide up from the bottom of the viewport and take turns: only one is open at a time. Drag the top edge of a drawer to change its height."],["terms",[["Library","The parts you can add. Opened with the {{Library}} button on the bottom toolbar or [[Shift + L]]. See [The library](#library)."],["Materials","Every material in the scene. Opened with [[Shift + M]]. See [Colours and materials](#colours-materials)."],["Console","A log of everything the app has done, with filters for information, warnings and errors and a button to copy it. Opened with the backtick key or the {{Console}} button in the status bar."]]],["h","Make room"],["p","Each sidebar has a drag handle on its inner edge. A thin line shows when you rest the pointer on it for a moment."],["list",["Drag a handle to make a sidebar wider or narrower. Double-click it to put the width back.","The left sidebar cannot be narrower than 220 pixels and the right one not narrower than 240. Narrower than that, buttons and labels would wrap or be cut off.","The window itself cannot be smaller than 960 by 560 pixels. In a smaller window the app scrolls instead of squeezing the panels.","Use the sidebar button in the top bar to hide the left sidebar.","On a narrow window, buttons of the bottom toolbar fold away into the `…` list."]],["h","Help"],["p","Press [[F1]] to open the help inside the app. It holds the same articles as this website and works without an internet connection. See [The help window](#help-window)."],["see",["help-window","move-the-view","parts-tree","search-and-commands","read-the-numbers"]]]},{"slug":"move-the-view","group":"around","title":"Move the view","summary":"Orbit, pan and zoom with the mouse, frame a part with F, and switch between the camera, Top, Front and Side views.","blocks":[["p","Moving the view never changes the model. You are only moving the camera."],["h","With the mouse"],["keys",[["Drag","Orbit: turn the view around the model (left mouse button)"],["Middle drag","Pan: slide the view sideways (press the wheel and drag)"],["Right drag","Pan as well"],["Scroll","Zoom in and out"],["Alt + Right drag","Zoom smoothly: drag up to come closer"]]],["h","Bring things into view"],["keys",[["F","Frame: fit the selected parts in the view, or the whole model when nothing is selected"]]],["p","If you get lost, click an empty spot to clear the selection and press [[F]]. The whole model comes back into view."],["h","Standard views"],["p","The pill at the top centre of the viewport names the current view. Click it to choose another."],["keys",[["Ctrl + 1","Cam: the free camera with perspective"],["Ctrl + 2","Top"],["Ctrl + 3","Front"],["Ctrl + 4","Side"]]],["p","Top, Front and Side are flat views without perspective, like a technical drawing. As soon as you orbit out of one, the app returns to the free camera from where you are."],["h","The view cube"],["p","The small cube in the top right corner turns with the model. Click one of its faces, or the dot at the end of an axis, to look straight along that axis: Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Side or Left. The view pill shows the name."],["h","The floor grid"],["p","The grid marks the floor of the scene. Press [[G]] to hide or show it."],["h","Settings that change how the view moves"],["p","Under {{Settings}}, {{Camera}}:"],["terms",[["Projection","Perspective, where far things look smaller, or Orthographic, where they do not."],["Field of view","How wide the lens is."],["Clip planes","How near and how far the camera can see. Leave it on Auto unless parts of a very small or very large model are cut off."],["Orbit around the selection","With something selected, the view turns around it. Off, or with nothing selected, it turns around the centre of the view."],["Zoom toward the cursor","The wheel zooms toward the pointer instead of the centre."]]],["note","On a heavy model, very small parts are left out while the view is moving and drawn again the moment it stops. This keeps movement smooth. You can turn it off under {{Settings}}, {{Performance}}, {{Skip tiny parts}}."],["see",["select-parts","view-modes","settings","shortcuts"]]]},{"slug":"select-parts","group":"around","title":"Select parts","summary":"Click, add with Shift, toggle with Ctrl, drag a box, or let the app select by shape, colour or name.","blocks":[["p","Most tools work on the parts you have selected. A selected part gets an outline in the viewport and its row is highlighted in the parts tree."],["h","In the viewport"],["keys",[["Click","Select one part. The tree scrolls to its row."],["Shift + Click","Add a part to the selection"],["Ctrl + Click","Add a part, or take it out if it was selected"],["Ctrl + Drag","Box select: draw a rectangle around parts"],["Ctrl + Shift + Drag","Box select and add to what is already selected"],["Esc","Select nothing. A click on empty space does the same."]]],["h","In the parts tree"],["keys",[["Click","Select one part. Click a group to select every part inside it."],["Ctrl + Click","Add or remove one row"],["Shift + Click","Select every row between the last one you clicked and this one"]]],["h","Let the app select"],["p","The card {{Selection & actions}} in the right sidebar has buttons for the common cases:"],["terms",[["All","Selects every visible part. [[Ctrl + A]]."],["Invert","Selects what was not selected, and the other way round. [[Ctrl + I]]."],["Clear","Selects nothing. [[Esc]]."],["Select similar","Adds every part that has the same shape as the selection. Select one bolt, press it, and all the bolts like it are selected."],["By colour","Selects every part that shares a colour with the selection."],["By name","Asks for a pattern and selects every part whose name matches it. Typing `screw` selects every part with screw in its name, in capitals or not. The pattern is a regular expression, a compact way to describe text, so some characters such as `.` and `*` have a special meaning."]]],["p","Two tools select for you in a cleverer way: [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts) and the size threshold of [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts)."],["h","Find what you selected"],["list",["Press [[Shift + S]] to scroll the tree to the selected part and open the groups it is in.","Press [[F]] to frame the selection in the viewport.","The status bar shows how many parts are selected. Click that number to frame them.","{{Properties}} shows the triangle count of the selection and its share of the scene."]],["h","The right-click menu"],["p","Right-click a part, in the viewport or in the tree, for a menu of things to do with the selection: frame it, isolate it, select similar parts, copy, duplicate or delete."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-context-menu.webp","Right-click a part to frame, isolate, select similar, copy or delete."],["note","A common mistake: dragging without [[Ctrl]] turns the view instead of drawing a selection box. Hold [[Ctrl]] first, then drag."],["see",["hide-isolate","parts-tree","search-and-commands"]]]},{"slug":"hide-isolate","group":"around","title":"Hide, isolate, show all","summary":"Hide parts that are in the way, look at a few parts on their own, and bring everything back with one key.","blocks":[["p","Hiding is how you look inside a model. A hidden part is still in the scene. It is only not drawn."],["keys",[["H","Hide the selected parts"],["Shift + H","Hide the rest: everything that is not selected"],["Alt + H","Show all parts again"],["S","Isolate the selection. Press it again to show everything."]]],["h","Isolate"],["steps",["Select the parts you want to look at.","Press [[S]]. Everything else disappears.","A pill at the top of the viewport says so, for example “Isolated · 1 of 43 parts”.","Press [[S]] again, or click the pill, to show everything."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-isolate.webp","A set of parts on their own, isolated."],["h","In the parts tree"],["p","Every row has an eye at its right end. Click it to hide or show that part. The eye on a group hides or shows everything inside it."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["Hiding and showing are undo steps. [[Ctrl + Z]] takes one back.","{{Properties}} shows how many parts are hidden.","A saved scene remembers which parts were hidden.","When you export, the switch {{Visible parts only}} decides whether hidden parts are written to the file. See [Export](#export)."]],["note","Hiding a part does not make the file lighter. To remove a part for good, delete it with [[Del]]."],["see",["select-parts","view-modes","select-hidden-parts"]]]},{"slug":"parts-tree","group":"around","title":"The parts tree","summary":"Search, rename, group and reorder the parts of a model, rename many at once, and flatten a hierarchy that is deeper than you need.","blocks":[["p","The parts tree in the left sidebar lists everything in the scene. A part is one row. A group is a folder that holds parts or other groups. A STEP file usually arrives with the groups its designer made."],["h","Read a row"],["list",["A part row shows its name and its triangle count.","A group row shows how many parts it holds. A group with nothing in it says “empty”.","The eye at the right hides or shows the row.","Click the small box in front of a group to open or close it."]],["h","The three buttons"],["terms",[["Collapse","Closes every group. When every group is closed, the button says {{Expand}} and opens them all. The small arrow beside it, or a right-click on the button, opens a menu with more choices. See below."],["Rename","Renames many parts at once. [[F2]]."],["Flatten","Removes levels of grouping."]]],["h","Collapse and expand part of the tree"],["p","A big assembly has many groups. Sometimes you want to close only some of them. Click the arrow beside {{Collapse}} to choose. Select a part or a group in the tree first, for the choices that need a selection."],["terms",[["Collapse the whole tree","Closes every group. The same as pressing {{Collapse}}."],["Collapse the selection","Closes the groups you selected and every group inside them. For a selected part, it closes the group the part is in."],["Collapse from here on","Closes the first selected row and every group after it in the tree. The groups above stay as they are, so the row you are on stays in view."],["Expand the whole tree","Opens every group."],["Expand the selection","Opens the groups you selected and every group inside them. For a selected part, it opens every group on the way down to it."],["Expand from here on","Opens the first selected row and every group after it."]]],["p","The choices that need a selection are dimmed while nothing is selected."],["h","Find the selected part"],["p","Picked a part in the 3D view and want to see where it sits in the tree? Press [[Shift + S]]. The tree scrolls to the part, opens the groups it is in and flashes its row. A click on a part in the 3D view also scrolls the tree to it."],["h","A file you add to the scene"],["p","When you add a file to a scene that is already open ({{Import into scene…}}, or a dropped file with {{Add to this scene}}), the new parts are selected, shown in the tree and framed in the 3D view. That tells you where they landed."],["h","Search"],["p","Type in {{Search parts…}} above the tree. Only the rows whose names match stay, including matches inside closed groups. Press the small cross in the field to clear the search."],["h","Rename one row"],["steps",["Double-click the name of a part or a group.","Type the new name.","Press [[Enter]]."]],["h","Rename many rows"],["p","CAD files often come with names like `Part_0031_geom`. Batch rename changes many names in one go."],["steps",["Select the parts to rename.","Press [[F2]] or the {{Rename}} button. The {{Batch rename}} window opens.","Choose a tab. {{Find & Replace}} swaps one piece of text for another. {{Pattern}} builds each name from pieces such as `{name}`, `{parent}` and `{counter:003}`. {{Presets}} has ready-made rules, such as lower-casing every name or removing a number at the end.","Look at the {{Preview}}. It shows every name before and after.","Press {{Apply}}, or [[Ctrl + Enter]]."]],["h","Group and ungroup"],["keys",[["Ctrl + G","Put the selected parts in a new group"],["Ctrl + Shift + G","Ungroup: remove the selected group and keep what was inside"],["Del","Delete the selected parts. With a group selected, the group and everything in it."]]],["p","A new group is called “Group” with a number. Double-click it to give it a name. Parts in a group move together when you move the group."],["h","Drag and drop"],["p","Drag a row onto a group to move it into that group, or between two rows to change the order. A group cannot be dropped into itself."],["h","Flatten"],["p","A STEP file can have groups inside groups ten levels deep. Flatten removes levels you do not need. Press {{Flatten}} to open the {{Advanced flatten}} window."],["terms",[["Scope","The whole tree, or only the groups you selected."],["Total flatten","Removes every group inside the scope. All parts end up side by side."],["Keep top N levels","Keeps the first levels of grouping and removes everything deeper."],["Flatten last level only","Removes only the deepest groups, the ones that hold nothing but parts."],["Collapse single-child chains","Removes groups that hold nothing but one other group. This is a common tidy-up for STEP files."],["Ungroup (dissolve scope itself)","Removes the selected groups and moves what they held one level up."],["Preserve user groups","Leaves alone the groups you made yourself."],["Remove empty groups left behind","Deletes groups that end up with nothing in them."]]],["h","The right-click menu"],["p","Right-click a part row for frame, isolate, hide, select similar, group, merge, Split, Fill holes, lock, Smart fit, copy, duplicate and delete. Right-click a group row to select its parts, frame it, rename it, ungroup it or delete it with its contents."],["p","A locked part cannot be moved, and delete skips it. Use the same menu entry to unlock it."],["h","Undo"],["p","Every change to the tree is an undo step: renames, groups, drags, flatten and deletes. Press [[Ctrl + Z]]."],["note","By default an emptied group stays in the tree, marked “empty”, so you can fill it again. To have such groups removed automatically, switch on {{Delete groups when they become empty}} under {{Settings}}, {{General}}."],["see",["select-parts","undo-redo","cleanup"]]]},{"slug":"search-and-commands","group":"around","title":"Search and commands","summary":"Press Ctrl + K to find any command, part or library part by name, and to work out a sum or convert a length.","blocks":[["p","You do not have to remember where a button is. The search finds it. It is the fastest way to use the app once you know roughly what you want."],["h","Open the search"],["steps",["Press [[Ctrl + K]], or click the magnifying glass at the left of the bottom toolbar.","Start typing. The list below narrows as you type.","Use [[↑]] and [[↓]] to choose a line.","Press [[Enter]] to run it. Press [[Esc]] to close the search without doing anything."]],["h","What it finds"],["terms",[["Commands","Everything in the menus and on the toolbars, each with its shortcut. Before you type, the commands you ran lately come first, with suggestions that fit what is selected."],["Sidebar controls","Every button, slider, switch and field in the right sidebar, listed under the name of its card. Choosing a button runs it. Choosing anything else opens its card and scrolls to it."],["Parts","Parts of the scene by name. [[Enter]] selects the part and frames it, even when it is hidden."],["Library parts","Parts you can add. Type `add flange` or just `flange`, and [[Enter]] adds one."]]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-search.webp","Search for “bolt”: the parts in the scene by name, and the bolts in the library ready to add."],["h","Commands worth knowing"],["p","Some tools have no button in the sidebar, or no key of their own. The search is the easy way to reach them. Type a few letters of the name:"],["terms",[["Smart optimise","Runs every clean-up in one go. See [Smart optimise](#smart-optimise)."],["Select fasteners…","Finds the bolts, screws, nuts and washers. See [Fasteners](#fasteners)."],["Find stacked copies…","Finds parts that sit on an identical copy of themselves. See [Stacked copies](#stacked-copies)."],["Select hidden parts…","Finds the parts that cannot be seen from outside. See [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts)."],["Align model to the floor…","Opens the card that stands the model on the floor. {{Align model to the floor (with the last choices)}} does it at once, without opening the card. See [Align to floor](#align-to-floor)."],["Dynamic place","Turns on dragging the selected part over other surfaces ([[D]]). See [Move, rotate and scale](#move-rotate-scale)."],["Fit to triangle budget","Takes you to the budget field so you can type a number."],["Optimisation report","Shows the model as it was opened against what it is now."],["Duplicate scene, Close other scenes, Close all scenes","Work on the scene tabs. See [Scene tabs](#scene-tabs)."],["Revert to source file…","Throws away every edit and loads the file again."],["Help & docs","Opens the help window ([[F1]]). See [The help window](#help-window)."],["Open welcome screen","Shows the start screen again."]]],["h","A calculator"],["p","Type a sum or a length and the search works it out. [[Enter]] copies the answer, ready to paste into a field."],["terms",[["12*25.4","A sum."],["2 in","A length in inches, given in millimetres."],["50 mm in in","A length converted to the unit you name."],["3 thou","Thousandths of an inch, given in millimetres."]]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-search-sum.webp","The search works out a sum or a length. Enter copies the answer."],["h","The list of all commands"],["p","The `…` button at the right end of the bottom toolbar opens every command that acts on the model in one list, with its shortcut. A command that cannot run right now is dimmed. Hover over it to see why, for example because nothing is selected."],["h","Command cards"],["p","Some commands have settings: Split, Fill holes, Smart fit, Select hidden parts, Fasteners, Stacked copies, Align to floor and Dynamic place. They open as a card over the bottom left of the viewport."],["keys",[["Enter","Run the open card"],["Esc","Put the card away. A second press clears the selection."]]],["h","Numbers you can drag"],["p","Any number field in the app can be dragged sideways to change it, or clicked to type a value. Hold [[Shift]] while dragging to go ten times faster and [[Alt]] to go ten times finer."],["see",["shortcuts","library","the-window"]]]},{"slug":"undo-redo","group":"around","title":"Undo and redo","summary":"Every editing action can be taken back with Ctrl + Z. What counts as one step, and how to go back to the file as it was opened.","blocks":[["keys",[["Ctrl + Z","Undo the last action"],["Ctrl + Y","Redo: do it again"],["Ctrl + Shift + Z","Redo as well"]]],["p","There are no undo buttons on screen. Undo and redo are keyboard only."],["h","What can be undone"],["p","Every editing action: deleting, hiding and showing, moving, renaming, grouping, flattening, recolouring, and every mesh tool such as Fill holes, Decimate, Smart fit and Split."],["h","What counts as one step"],["list",["A tool that changes many parts at once is one step. One [[Ctrl + Z]] takes back a whole Fill holes run or a whole Fit to budget.","In Align to floor, a run of adjustments is one step.","Changing the threshold of Delete small parts while its parts are isolated adds no step of its own."]],["h","Each tab has its own history"],["p","Undo works on the scene in the tab you are looking at. Other tabs are not touched. See [Scene tabs](#scene-tabs)."],["h","Start over"],["p","To throw away every edit and reload the file as it was, choose {{Menu}}, then {{Revert to source…}}. The app asks before it does this."],["note","Your original file on disk is never changed by anything you do in the app. Edits reach a file only when you save a scene or export."],["note","After a change that is too large to keep an undo copy of, the app clears the history and tells you so. This is rare. Save a scene before a very heavy operation if you want a way back."],["see",["save-scene","scene-tabs","shortcuts"]]]},{"slug":"scene-tabs","group":"around","title":"Scene tabs","summary":"Each tab is a scene of its own. Open several files side by side, drop a file to add it or open it in a tab, drag tabs into order, and save from the tab’s dot.","blocks":[["p","A scene is everything you are working on in one tab: its parts, materials, camera and undo history. The tabs sit in the top bar, like tabs in a browser. Each one is a separate scene."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-tabs.webp","Scene tabs: drag them into order. The dot on the icon is yellow while there are unsaved changes."],["h","Open and close"],["list",["Click the `+` after the last tab, or press [[Ctrl + N]], for a new empty scene.","Opening a file never replaces a scene that has something in it. The file opens in a new tab.","Click a tab to switch to it. Nothing is reloaded.","Click the cross at the right end of a tab to close it. A click with the middle mouse button closes it too.","If the scene has changes that are not saved, the app asks before it closes the tab."]],["h","Drop a file on the window"],["p","You can drop a file anywhere on the window. What happens depends on the scene you have open."],["terms",[["The scene is empty","The file opens there."],["The scene has parts in it","A small question comes up: {{Add to this scene}} or {{Open in new tab}}. Add puts the parts of the file in the scene you are looking at, next to what is there. Open in new tab makes a scene of its own. Press [[Esc]] or click outside the question to do nothing."]]],["p","The {{Import into scene…}} command in the menu ([[Ctrl + Shift + O]]) always adds to the open scene. After an add, the new parts are selected, so you can see where they landed."],["h","Read a tab"],["terms",[["Dot","The first thing in every tab. Yellow means there are changes that are not saved. Green means the scene is saved. Faint means the scene is empty. Click the dot of the open tab to save the scene. It does the same as [[Ctrl + S]]."],["Name","The name of the scene. Double-click it to rename the scene. A long name is cut in the middle, so the end of it and the type of the file stay readable."],["Cross","Closes the tab."]]],["p","Every tab has the same width, and nothing in a tab moves when you pick it."],["h","Right-click a tab"],["p","A menu opens with the things you can do with tabs:"],["terms",[["Duplicate scene","Opens a copy of that tab’s scene in a new tab. It is dimmed when the scene is empty."],["Close all","Closes every tab. [[Ctrl + Shift + F4]]."],["Close others","Closes every tab except this one."],["Open…","Opens a file, as [[Ctrl + O]] does."],["Open recent","A list of the files you opened lately."]]],["p","Close all and Close others ask just once for all the scenes that have unsaved changes. Duplicate scene, Close all and Close others are in the search ([[Ctrl + K]]) too."],["h","Put tabs in order"],["steps",["Press a tab and pull it sideways.","The tab rides with the pointer and the others slide over to make room.","Let go. It drops into the nearest place."]],["p","Press [[Esc]] while you are still holding the tab to put it back where it was."],["h","Sidebars across tabs"],["p","When you switch tabs, the widths of the sidebars and the folded left sidebar stay as they were in the tab you came from. To let each tab keep its own layout, switch off {{Same sidebars in every tab}} under {{Settings}}, {{General}}."],["see",["save-scene","first-file","undo-redo"]]]},{"slug":"settings","group":"around","title":"Settings","summary":"One window with eight sections and a search. What each section holds, in a sentence or two, from the start-up options to the look of the interface.","blocks":[["p","Open it with [[Ctrl + ,]] or {{Menu}}, then {{Settings…}}. Changes apply as you make them. There is nothing to confirm: press {{Done}} or [[Esc]] to close the window."],["p","The list at the left has the sections. The field at the top searches every section at once. Press [[Ctrl + F]] to jump to it. While a search is typed, [[Esc]] clears it first. At the bottom of the list are links to {{Help & docs}} and {{Keyboard shortcuts}}, and the version number."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-settings-general.webp","Settings, General."],["h","General"],["p","How the app starts and behaves."],["terms",[["Splash screen","The short splash with the version while the app opens. Off: the app just fades in."],["Start screen","Show the start screen when the app opens."],["Offer the last file","A Resume button on the start screen for the file you had open last."],["Fit the view after loading","Move the camera so a newly opened model fills the view."],["Same sidebars in every tab","Keep the sidebar widths when you switch tabs."],["Confirm destructive actions","Ask before deletes and other steps that are hard to take back."],["Warn before leaving with unsaved changes","Closing or reloading the window asks first when a scene has changes that are not saved. On by default."],["Undo history","How many steps [[Ctrl + Z]] can go back: {{Short}} (50), {{Normal}} (200) or {{Long}} (500). A longer history uses more memory. See [Undo and redo](#undo-redo)."],["Delete groups when they become empty","Remove a group automatically when an action empties it. Off by default."]]],["h","Appearance"],["p","The look of the interface. It does not change the model or the 3D view. {{Reset}} at the bottom puts everything in this section back to the defaults."],["terms",[["Accent","The colour of buttons, selections and highlights. Choose one of nine colours, or the last swatch to pick any colour."],["Surfaces","The greys the interface is built from: {{Graphite}}, {{Midnight}}, {{Warm}} or {{Black}}."],["Font","The typeface of the interface: {{Inter}}, {{System}}, {{Mono}} or {{Serif}}."],["Text size","Everything written in the interface, from {{Small}} to {{Larger}}. Buttons and fields grow with it."],["Corners","How round the corners of buttons and cards are: {{Sharp}}, {{Default}} or {{Round}}."],["Density","The height of buttons, fields and dropdowns: {{Compact}}, {{Default}} or {{Roomy}}."],["Interface animations","Fades, slides and the movement of the splash screen. Off: everything just appears."]]],["h","Viewport"],["p","What the 3D view draws around the model."],["terms",[["Highlight small parts","Tints the parts that are under the size threshold of Delete small parts."],["CAD view look","The look the CAD view (key [[6]]) shades with: {{Ceramic}}, {{Light}} or {{Mono}}. The same choice is in the {{Shading}} card on the right."],["Outlines","A fine dark line on the sharp edges of every part, in every view. On by default. The same switch is in the {{Shading}} card."],["Ground grid","The floor grid. [[G]] switches it too."],["Sun direction gizmo","A handle in the viewport for turning the light."],["FPS counter and Renderer stats","Small read-outs of how fast the view is drawing."],["Show origin points","Shows a dot at the origin of each group in the viewport. Off by default."],["Fog","Fades the grid and distant parts into the background, with start, end and intensity."],["Background","Dark, Studio gradient, Neutral grey, White, or HDRI environment. An HDRI is a picture of surroundings that lights the model. There are four built-in ones (Studio, Outdoor, Sunset, Overcast), or load your own `.hdr` or `.exr` file."]]],["h","Camera"],["p","Projection, field of view, clip planes, and how the view moves."],["terms",[["Projection","Perspective, as a camera sees, or Orthographic, with no perspective."],["Field of view","How wide the lens is, in perspective."],["Clip planes","How near and how far the camera sees. {{Auto}} fits the scene. {{Tight}} is for tiny parts, {{Wide}} for large assemblies and {{Huge}} for the size of a factory."],["Orbit around the selection","With something selected, the view turns around it. Off, or with nothing selected: around the centre of the view."],["Zoom toward the cursor","The wheel zooms toward the pointer instead of the centre of the view."],["Orbit speed and Zoom speed","How far the view turns for a drag and moves for the wheel: {{Slow}}, {{Normal}} or {{Fast}}."]]],["p","The mouse moves are explained in [Move the view](#move-the-view)."],["h","Performance"],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-settings-performance.webp","Settings, Performance: the renderer, quality, and what a moving view gives up."],["terms",[["Renderer","WebGPU, or WebGL2 where the browser or the graphics driver has no WebGPU. Changing it reloads the page."],["Quality","Auto adapts to the size of the scene. High always draws at full sharpness. Low draws coarser, for scenes with millions of triangles."],["Auto-instance duplicates","Draw repeated shapes as one mesh with many positions. It applies the next time a model is loaded."],["Share materials by colour","Let parts of the same colour share one material. It applies the next time a model is loaded."],["Skip tiny parts","While the view moves, leave out parts that would be only a few pixels across."],["Lower the resolution if it stutters","While a heavy scene moves, draw it a little coarser, and sharp again when it stops."]]],["h","Scene"],["p","These belong to the open scene and are saved with it. [[Ctrl + ;]] opens this section directly, and so does {{Scene settings…}} in the menu."],["terms",[["Display units","The unit used for sizes and distances on screen: millimetres, centimetres, metres, inches, feet, or none."],["Up axis (viewport)","Which direction is up: Z, as CAD programs have it, or Y, as glTF and some 3D programs have it. It changes how the scene is shown. The exported file follows the choice in the Export window."],["Scene scale (×)","A multiplier for the whole scene on screen. 0.001 turns millimetres into metres."],["Cell size","The size of a grid cell. {{Auto}} fits the model."],["Snap gizmo to grid","The move tool jumps from one grid cell to the next."]]],["p","The light has no settings here. The sun is turned with the {{Sun direction gizmo}} in the viewport."],["h","Storage"],["p","What the app remembers on this computer, each with a button to clear it:"],["terms",[["Recent files","The list on the start screen."],["File access","Permissions that let the app reopen a recent file without asking where it is."],["Import options","Shows the options window again for the formats where you ticked “Don’t ask again”."],["Panel sizes","The widths of the sidebars and the heights of the drawers."],["All settings","Puts every preference back to its default, the look of the interface included."]]],["h","About"],["p","The version, where to find help, and what to send when something goes wrong."],["terms",[["Details","The version of the app, the renderer, how many parts and triangles the scene has, the size of your screen and your browser."],["Help & docs, Keyboard shortcuts","Open the help window and the list of shortcuts. See [The help window](#help-window)."],["Copy details","Copies the details above, with your graphics card and the look you chose, as text. Paste it into a new issue on GitHub so the problem is easier to find."],["Report an issue","Opens a new issue on GitHub in your browser."],["Website, Source code","The downloads and docs, and the code on GitHub. The app is free and open source, under the MIT licence."]]],["see",["move-the-view","view-modes","privacy-offline","help-window"]]]},{"slug":"help-window","group":"around","title":"The help window","summary":"These articles also live inside the app. Press F1 to open them, with a search, and read them with no internet connection.","blocks":[["p","You do not have to leave the app to find an answer. The help window holds the same articles as this website. Its text is stored inside the app, so it opens even when you are offline."],["h","Open it"],["list",["Press [[F1]].","Or choose {{Menu}}, then {{Help & docs}}.","Or click the app’s mark at the far left of the top bar, then {{Help & docs}}.","Or open {{Settings}} and click {{Help & docs}} in the list at the left.","On the start screen there is a {{Help}} link at the top."]],["p","The window opens on the article you read last. The first time, it opens on the first one. The command {{Help & docs}} is in the search ([[Ctrl + K]]) too."],["h","Find your way"],["terms",[["The list at the left","Every article, in groups. Click one to read it."],["The article","At the right. At its end, {{Previous}} and {{Next}} take you to the neighbours. A link inside an article opens the other article in the same window. A {{Copy}} button next to a command copies it."],["The search field","At the top. Type a word or two. The list changes into the articles that contain them, best match first, with a few words around the hit. [[Enter]] opens the first one. The words are marked in the article."],["Website","A button at the top right. It opens the same pages on the website, which is where the pictures live."]]],["keys",[["F1","Open the help window"],["/","Jump to the search field, while you are not typing"],["Enter","In the search field: open the first result"],["Esc","Clear the search. Press it again to close the window"]]],["h","Good to know"],["list",["The pictures are loaded from the website. Without an internet connection the text is all there, and the pictures are left out.","The text is a copy made when your version of the app was built. The website can be newer.","The help window does not change anything in your scene. Close it and you are back where you were."]],["see",["the-window","search-and-commands","shortcuts","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"read-the-numbers","group":"lighter","title":"How to read the numbers","summary":"What a triangle count tells you, where the app shows it, how to find the heaviest parts, and how to see what your work has saved.","blocks":[["p","A 3D model on a screen is made of triangles. Every triangle has to be stored in the file and drawn many times a second. So the triangle count is the simplest measure of how heavy a model is: fewer triangles mean a smaller file and a smoother view."],["p","There is no single right number. It depends on where the model will be shown. Ask whoever will use the file what they need, then work towards that."],["h","The whole scene"],["p","With nothing selected, {{Properties}} in the right sidebar describes the scene:"],["terms",[["The large number","Triangles in the scene (“tris”)."],["% of original","How many triangles are left compared with the file as it was opened. It starts at 100%."],["per part","The average number of triangles in a part."],["Parts","How many parts there are."],["Vertices","How many corner points the triangles have."],["Size","How large the whole model is along its three axes, in the display unit."],["Mesh data","How much memory the triangles take. A shape that repeats is counted once."]]],["p","The status bar under the viewport shows the number of vertices, the size of the mesh data, how many parts are selected and flagged, the frame rate and the console."],["h","One part"],["p","Select a part and {{Properties}} describes it instead: its name, its material, its triangle count, its share of the scene, and where it ranks by weight, for example “#28 of 67 by weight”. Below that are its vertices, size and volume."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-inspect.webp","One part selected: its triangles, its share of the scene and its rank by weight."],["p","Every part remembers the triangle count it arrived with. After you have reduced a part, the card leads with what was saved, in the form “21,688 triangles −34% · was 32,728 · saved 11,040”."],["h","What an action saved"],["p","Right after an action that removes triangles, the saving shows beside the scene’s triangle count for a few seconds, for example “−427,543 (−38%)”."],["h","Find the heavy parts"],["steps",["Open the card {{Heavy parts}} in the right sidebar.","It lists the parts with the most triangles, heaviest first, each with a bar that shows how it compares with the heaviest.","Click a row to tick it: the part is selected in the viewport. Right-click a row to move the view to the part. The card {{Heavy parts}} lists the parts with more than 10,000 triangles.","Decide what to do with it: delete it, simplify it, or leave it."]],["p","A few parts often hold a large share of the triangles. Start with those."],["h","Heatmap"],["p","Press [[4]] for the heatmap view. Each part is coloured by how densely it is covered in triangles for its size. Dense parts stand out in warm colours. A small part in a hot colour carries far more detail than it needs. Press [[1]] to go back to the normal view."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-heatmap.webp","Heatmap: parts coloured by how dense their triangles are."],["h","The optimisation report"],["p","The report puts the scene as it was opened beside the scene as it is now. It is the number to show whoever asked for a lighter file."],["steps",["Open the card {{Reduce triangles}} and press {{Optimisation report}}. The search ([[Ctrl + K]]) finds it too.","Read the table: {{Original}}, {{Now}} and {{Change}} for triangles, vertices, parts, draw calls, materials and mesh data in memory.","The row {{File}} fills in after you have exported.","Press {{Copy}} to copy the table as text."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-report.webp","The optimisation report: the scene as it was opened beside the scene now."],["see",["workflow","delete-small-parts","fit-to-budget","view-modes"]]]},{"slug":"heavy-parts","group":"lighter","title":"Heavy parts","summary":"A list of the parts that hold the most triangles, heaviest first, with a count beside its title. Tick the ones you do not need and delete them in one step.","blocks":[["p","A few parts often hold a large share of a model’s triangles. The card {{Heavy parts}} lists them, so you can see where the weight is without searching the tree."],["h","What the card lists"],["p","Only parts with more than 10,000 triangles are listed. The heaviest is at the top, and the list holds at most 500 parts. Each row shows the part’s place in the list, its name, its triangle count (for example 12.4k) and a bar that compares it with the heaviest part. Hover over a row to see the full name, the exact count and the rank."],["p","The card is in the right sidebar, under Inspect. Beside its title is a small number: how many parts are over the limit. You can read it while the card is folded. It goes down as you delete or reduce parts, and it reads 0, dimmed, when no part is that big."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Heavy parts}} in the right sidebar, under Inspect.","Click a row to tick it. The part is selected in the viewport. Click the row again to take the tick off. Rows you ticked before stay ticked.","To tick a run of rows, click the first one, then hold [[Shift]] and click the last.","To see where a part is, right-click its row. The view moves to the part. The ticks stay as they are.","Press the button under the list. It says what it will remove, for example “Delete 3 parts · 171.3k triangles”. It cannot be pressed until a row is ticked.","If you removed too much, press [[Ctrl + Z]]. The whole delete is one undo step."]],["note","A tick is the same thing as selecting the part. A part that you selected in the viewport or in the tree is ticked in the list too, if it is listed. The button deletes only the ticked rows, never a selected part that is not on the list."],["h","What to do with a heavy part"],["list",["Delete it, if nobody will miss it. Deleting costs no quality.","Reduce it with [Decimate](#decimate), if it matters and is simply too dense.","Replace it with a simple stand-in using [Smart fit](#smart-fit), if only its size and place matter.","Leave it, if it is the part everybody will look at."]],["h","Good to know"],["list",["The list follows the scene. After a delete, an undo or a decimate it updates a moment later.","The limit of 10,000 triangles is fixed. A scene made of many lighter parts can be heavy without one part in the list. To bring a whole scene down to a number, use [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget) or [Smart optimise](#smart-optimise).","For a picture of where the density is, press [[4]] for the heatmap. See [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers)."]],["see",["read-the-numbers","decimate","smart-fit","fit-to-budget"]]]},{"slug":"smart-optimise","group":"lighter","title":"Smart optimise","summary":"One card, one button: every clean-up the app has, run one after another on the whole scene. Choose how far to go, read the plan, press Optimise.","blocks":[["p","The other tools in this group each do one job. Smart optimise runs them for you, one after another, on the whole scene. Use it when you want a lighter model and do not want to choose the order yourself."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-smart-optimise.webp","Smart optimise at the top of Reduce. Auto picked Balanced, and the line under the controls says what the run will do."],["p","It is lossy: it removes parts and triangles. The whole run is one undo step, so one [[Ctrl + Z]] takes everything back."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open a model.","Open the card {{Smart optimise}} in the right sidebar, at the top of Reduce.","Choose how far to go: {{Auto}}, {{Light}}, {{Balanced}} or {{Strong}}. Auto is the default.","Look at {{Triangle target}}. Type another number if you need one, for example `300k` or `1.5m`.","Read the line under the controls. It spells out what the run will do, in order. With Auto it also says which level was chosen, for example “Auto picked Balanced for 1,103,373 triangles in 43 parts”.","Press {{Optimise}}.","When it is done, a message gives the triangles before and after and how many parts were removed.","Look at the model. If it lost too much, press [[Ctrl + Z]] once and try a lighter level."]],["p","You can also run it from the search: press [[Ctrl + K]] and type `Smart optimise`. It runs with whatever the card is set to."],["h","The levels"],["p","A level is a starting point. It sets the switches under {{Steps}} and the triangle target."],["terms",[["Auto","Chooses from how heavy the scene is: under 200,000 triangles it uses Light, under 2 million it uses Balanced, and above that Strong. The line under the controls says what it chose."],["Light","Only tidies. It removes empty parts, parts whose triangles have no area, and empty groups. No triangles are reduced."],["Balanced","Also removes parts hidden inside the model, turns bolts, nuts and washers into plain cylinders, removes parts smaller than 1% of the model, and reduces the scene to half its triangles."],["Strong","Like Balanced, but it removes parts smaller than 2%, also closes holes in flat faces, and reduces the scene to a quarter of its triangles."]]],["h","The steps"],["p","Open {{Steps}} in the card to see each switch. If you change one, no level is lit any more: the settings are yours. Each step is the same tool you could run by hand."],["terms",[["Remove empty and broken parts","Parts with no triangles, parts whose triangles have no area, and groups with nothing left in them. See [Auto cleanup and Tidy geometry](#cleanup)."],["Remove parts hidden inside","Parts that cannot be seen from outside, from any direction. See [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts)."],["Bolts, nuts, washers","Found by their shape, up to a thread of M30, as in [Fasteners](#fasteners). {{Keep}} leaves them alone. {{Simplify}} turns each into a plain cylinder. {{Delete}} removes them."],["Remove parts under","Parts whose box, measured from corner to corner, is shorter than this share of the whole model. 0 leaves small parts alone. See [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts)."],["Fill holes","Closes holes in flat faces, with the settings and the size limit of the Fill holes panel. See [Fill holes](#fill-holes)."],["Reduce triangles","A switch of its own. On: the densest meshes are reduced until the whole scene has the number of triangles in {{Triangle target}}, as in [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget). Off: the run only removes and tidies, and every mesh that stays keeps all its triangles. If the target is not lower than the scene has now, nothing is reduced."],["Triangle target","How many triangles the whole scene may have after the run. You can write `150000`, `150k` or `1.5m`. Choosing a level sets it from the scene you have now. A number you type is kept."]]],["p","The steps always run in this order: tidy up, hidden parts, fasteners, small parts, holes, then the reduction. Removing comes before reducing on purpose, so no effort goes into thinning a part that is deleted a moment later."],["h","While it runs"],["p","A progress card names the step that is running. {{Cancel}} stops the run before the next step and keeps what has already been done. Each step writes a line to the Console (the button at the bottom of the window), and one summary is shown at the end."],["h","Undo"],["p","The whole run is one undo step. [[Ctrl + Z]] takes all of it back, and redo does all of it again."],["note","Start with Light or Balanced and look at the result. Undo is one key press, so you can try Strong afterwards at no cost. Do not use the removing steps when someone will open a cover, look through a window in the casing, or need the fasteners. Switch those steps off, or use the single tools one at a time."],["see",["workflow","fasteners","select-hidden-parts","delete-small-parts","fill-holes","fit-to-budget"]]]},{"slug":"delete-small-parts","group":"lighter","title":"Delete small parts","summary":"Remove the screws, washers and clips that are too small to matter, with a slider and a preview of what will go.","blocks":[["p","A CAD assembly is mostly small things: screws, washers, clips, nuts. From a normal viewing distance nobody sees them, but there are hundreds of them and each one costs triangles. This card removes every part below a size you choose."],["h","When to use it"],["p","Early, when the model is for looking at as a whole and the small hardware does not matter. Do not use it when the fasteners are the point of the picture."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Delete small parts}} in the right sidebar, under Clean up.","Drag the slider {{Smaller than}}. The value is a share of the whole model’s size. It starts at 2%.","Read the line under it. It says how many parts would go and how long that is, for example “710 parts under 70.5 mm corner to corner”.","Press {{Isolate}}. Only the parts that would be deleted are shown, so you can check them.","Drag the slider again if needed. While the small parts are isolated, the view follows the slider.","Press {{Delete}}. The button says how many small parts it will remove.","Press {{Show all}} to see the rest of the model again."]],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Smaller than","The threshold, in percent of the model’s size. A part below it counts as small."],["Measured by: Diagonal","The distance from corner to corner of the part’s bounding box. The usual choice."],["Measured by: Longest side","The part’s longest side. It keeps long thin parts such as rods and pipes, and is stricter with flat ones."],["Measured by: Volume","The volume of the part’s bounding box. It catches thin sheets and wires however long they are."]]],["h","Hidden parts are left alone"],["p","Only parts that are on show are looked at: the ones with an open eye in the parts tree. A part you hid is not counted, not isolated and not deleted. If there are hidden parts, the line under the slider says how many it skipped, for example “3 hidden parts are left alone”. Hiding or showing a part updates the count at once. While the small parts are isolated, the card goes on choosing from the parts that were on show before you pressed {{Isolate}}."],["h","See the small parts without deleting them"],["p","The button with the warning triangle on the viewport’s tool strip tints every part under the threshold. The same switch is {{Highlight small parts}} under {{Settings}}, {{Viewport}}."],["h","Undo"],["p","The delete is one undo step. Press [[Ctrl + Z]] and the parts are back."],["note","A common mistake is a threshold that is too high. At a few percent, handles, knobs, feet and hinges count as small too. Always press {{Isolate}} and look before you delete."],["p","To remove only the hardware, and not every small part, use [Fasteners](#fasteners). [Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) has this step too, under the name {{Remove parts under}}."],["see",["select-hidden-parts","fasteners","smart-optimise","read-the-numbers","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"select-hidden-parts","group":"lighter","title":"Select hidden parts","summary":"Find every part that cannot be seen from outside the model, such as screws inside a housing, and delete them in one step.","blocks":[["p","Much of an assembly is inside it: the screws in a housing, the board under a cover, a bearing in its seat. If the model is only ever seen from outside, those parts are dead weight. This command looks at the model from every side and selects the parts that never show."],["p","On the rear derailleur used in these pictures it found one part, 3,160 triangles, tucked inside the housing. On a bigger assembly there are usually many more."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-select-hidden-result.webp","One part inside the housing can’t be seen from any side: 3,160 triangles, selected."],["h","When to use it"],["p","When the model will be shown closed, from outside. Not when someone will open a cover, look through a window in the casing, or show an exploded view."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Click the {{Select hidden parts}} button on the bottom toolbar. You can also find it in the `…` list or with the search. Its panel opens at the bottom left of the viewport.","Leave {{Whole scene}} chosen.","Press {{Select hidden parts}}, or [[Enter]].","The hidden parts are selected. {{Properties}} shows how many triangles they hold.","Press {{Isolate}} in the panel to look at them on their own. Check that nothing you need is among them.","Press {{Delete}} in the panel to remove them."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-select-hidden.webp","Select hidden parts: the command panel beside the viewport."],["note","The command only selects. Nothing is deleted until you press {{Delete}}."],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Whole scene / Selection only","Check every visible part, or only the parts you selected. With Selection only, the rest of the scene still counts as something that hides them."],["Accuracy","Under {{Advanced}}. How many directions the scene is looked at from: Fast, Normal or Thorough. More directions find parts that show only through a narrow gap, and take longer."],["See-through parts hide what is behind","Off: a see-through part, such as glass or a clear cover, hides nothing, so what is behind it counts as visible. On: it hides what is behind it like any other part."],["Add to the selection","Keeps what is selected now and adds the hidden parts to it."],["Largest part, of model size","Keeps only hidden parts up to this share of the model’s size. Empty means any size."],["Name contains","Keeps only hidden parts whose name contains this text, such as `screw` or `bearing`. Empty means any name."]]],["h","Undo"],["p","Selecting changes nothing. The delete is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]]."],["h","Tips"],["list",["Show all parts first ([[Alt + H]]). The check looks at visible parts.","If a part that you can see through a small opening was selected, run it again with {{Accuracy}} on Thorough.","To remove only the hidden fasteners and keep hidden structure, type part of their name in {{Name contains}}, or set a small value in {{Largest part, of model size}}."]],["p","[Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) runs this command as one of its steps, {{Remove parts hidden inside}}, with its usual settings."],["see",["delete-small-parts","fasteners","smart-optimise","hide-isolate","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"fasteners","group":"lighter","title":"Fasteners","summary":"Find every bolt, screw, nut and washer by its shape, whatever the parts are called, then isolate or delete them in one step.","blocks":[["p","A machine can hold hundreds of fasteners: the small metal parts that hold other parts together. In a CAD export their names are often useless, for example `=>0_1_1_9_000009`, so searching by name does not work. Fasteners looks at the shape of every part instead, and selects the ones shaped like a bolt, a screw, a nut or a washer."],["p","It cuts each part across its own axis and reads the outline: a round shank with a wider head, six flats around a hole, a flat ring. It compares that with the proportions of standard fasteners. Bushings, bearings, O-rings, shafts, pipe fittings and cable glands have other shapes, and are left alone."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-fasteners.webp","Fasteners found twelve bolts and screws by their shape: 129,460 triangles, selected and ready to isolate or delete."],["h","When to use it"],["p","Before you delete or simplify the hardware, when the names do not help. Not when the fasteners are the point of the picture."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Click the {{Fasteners}} button (a bolt) on the bottom toolbar. You can also find it in the `…` list, or with the search ([[Ctrl + K]], “Select fasteners”). Its panel opens at the bottom left of the viewport.","Set {{up to M}} to the largest thread to look for. It starts at 30. A thread size is written M and the width in millimetres: M6 is a bolt 6 mm wide. A bolt, nut or washer for a bigger thread is left alone.","Leave the three switches on: {{Bolts and screws}}, {{Nuts}} and {{Washers}}. Switch off a kind you do not want.","Press {{Select fasteners}}, or [[Enter]]. It looks at every visible part. On a large assembly it shows its progress and takes a few seconds.","The fasteners are selected. A line says how many, how many triangles they hold and what share of the scene that is, for example “12 fasteners selected: 129,460 triangles, 11.7% of the scene”.","Under it is a short list with one row per kind and the sizes found, for example “12 bolts and screws · M3.5 ×6 · M4 ×4 · M5 ×2”. Click a row to select only that kind. Click it again to go back to all of them.","Press {{Isolate}} to look at them on their own. Check that nothing you need is among them.","Press {{Delete}} to remove them, or leave them selected and use another tool on them, such as [Smart fit](#smart-fit)."]],["note","The command only selects. Nothing is deleted until you press {{Delete}}."],["h","What the options mean"],["p","Open {{Advanced}} in the panel."],["terms",[["Look in: Whole scene / Selection","Check every visible part, or only the parts you selected first."],["How sure: Certain","Only parts with a clear fastener shape: a hex head, a key socket, a hex nut, a washer of standard proportions."],["How sure: Likely too","The default. Also plain two-cylinder screws and headed pins, which have the proportions of a fastener and nothing else to show for it."],["Pins, studs and threaded rods","Plain round bars up to the size limit. Off by default, because a shaft or a roller is a round bar too."],["Add to the selection","Keeps what is selected now and adds the fasteners to it."],["Used at least … times","Takes a fastener only if the scene holds at least this many of the same shape and size. Fasteners come in sets. 1 takes single ones too."],["Smallest thread","Leaves the fasteners for threads below this alone. 0 means no lower limit."],["Reset to defaults","Puts every option back."]]],["h","Sizes and units"],["p","The app works in millimetres. A file saved in metres, centimetres or inches would show its fasteners at impossible sizes. The app checks which unit gives the sizes of real fasteners and reads the file that way. When it had to, the line under the button says so, for example “Sizes read as inches”."],["h","Simplify instead of delete"],["p","To keep a stand-in where each fastener was, leave them selected and run [Smart fit](#smart-fit) with {{Cylinder}}. [Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) does the same with its {{Simplify}} choice."],["h","Undo"],["p","Selecting changes nothing. The delete is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]]."],["h","Tips"],["list",["Show all parts first ([[Alt + H]]). Only visible parts are looked at.","If a part that is not a fastener was selected, press {{Certain}} under {{How sure}} and run it again.","If a fastener was missed, raise {{up to M}}, or switch on {{Pins, studs and threaded rods}}."]],["see",["select-hidden-parts","delete-small-parts","smart-fit","smart-optimise"]]]},{"slug":"stacked-copies","group":"lighter","title":"Stacked copies","summary":"Find parts that sit exactly on top of an identical copy of themselves, drawn twice, and remove the extra ones.","blocks":[["p","Some files hold the same part twice, in the same place. From a distance nothing looks wrong. But the two surfaces lie on each other, so the screen flickers where they meet, because the graphics card cannot tell which one is in front. And the triangles are stored and drawn twice. Stacked copies finds these parts and keeps one of each."],["p","Ten identical bolts in ten different places are not stacked copies. Only parts in the same place count."],["h","Open the card"],["p","Press [[Ctrl + K]] to open the search, type `stacked`, and choose {{Find stacked copies…}}. The card opens over the viewport, at the bottom left, like [Split](#split) and [Fill holes](#fill-holes). [[Esc]] puts it away."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Stacked copies}} as above.","Set {{Same place within}}. The slider goes from 0 to 5 percent of the part’s size, and starts at 0.5. At 0 the parts must lie almost exactly on each other. A higher value also accepts copies that are a little off.","Switch on {{Also near copies}} if the copies may differ a little. Off, two parts count as copies only when they have the same number of triangles. On, the number may differ by up to a quarter, as with another export of the same part, or a copy that was rebuilt with a different mesh.","Press {{Find stacked copies}}.","The extra copies are selected, and a line says how many, for example “6 extra copies of 6 parts selected; the first of each is kept”. The part that was loaded first stays. If there are none, it says “No stacked copies found”.","Press {{Isolate}} to look at the extra copies on their own. Check them.","Press {{Delete}} to remove them."]],["note","The command only selects. Nothing is deleted until you press {{Delete}}."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["Hidden parts are not looked at. Show all parts first ([[Alt + H]]).","The card remembers {{Same place within}} and {{Also near copies}} for next time.","Faces that lie exactly on each other still flicker in the viewport. This card is the way to find the parts that cause it."]],["h","Undo"],["p","Selecting changes nothing. The delete is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]] brings the copies back."],["see",["select-hidden-parts","cleanup","delete-small-parts","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"fill-holes","group":"lighter","title":"Fill holes","summary":"Close bolt holes, slots, pockets and lettering in flat faces. Each closed hole removes the triangles of its walls.","blocks":[["p","A plate with forty bolt holes carries forty small cylinders of triangles that nobody notices from a distance. Fill holes closes holes in flat faces and leaves the rest of each part exactly as it was. The part keeps its outline and loses the triangles inside the holes."],["p","With the limit at 12 mm it closes bolt holes, slots and pockets up to that size, and leaves bosses and the bore of a washer or a nut alone."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-fill-holes.webp","Fill holes: closes bolt holes, slots and pockets in flat faces and leaves the rest alone."],["h","What it closes, and what it leaves"],["list",["Closed: holes that go right through, holes with a floor, counterbores, slots, pockets and engraved lettering in flat faces.","Left alone: a boss or a pin that stands up from a face.","Left alone: an opening that is what the part is, such as the bore of a washer, a nut or a bushing.","Left alone: an opening that leads into a hollow space or out through a curved surface.","Left alone: holes in curved surfaces. The tool works on flat faces only.","Left alone: instanced parts, which the file stores as repeats of one shared shape. The result line says how many it passed over."]],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the parts to work on. With nothing selected, it works on every visible part.","Press [[P]], or click {{Fill holes}} on the bottom toolbar. The panel opens at the bottom left of the viewport.","Set {{up to}} to the largest hole you want closed, in millimetres, measured across the hole’s widest point. It starts at 12.","Press {{Fill holes}}, or [[Enter]].","The holes close. The app reports how many, and the saving shows beside the triangle count."]],["h","What the advanced options mean"],["p","Open {{Advanced}} in the panel."],["terms",[["Through holes","Holes that go right through: an opening on both sides."],["Blind holes and pockets","Holes and pockets with a floor: an opening on one side only."],["Open holes","Openings with nothing behind them: a missing patch in a flat face."],["Raised lettering and logos","Lettering and marks that stand proud of a flat face are flattened into it. Bosses, pins and ribs are taller than the limit and stay."],["Raised, up to","A raised detail is flattened only if it stands no higher than this above its face. Embossed type is usually 0.1 to 0.5 mm high."],["Keep openings that shape the part","On: an opening that takes up much of its face and of the part is kept, such as the bore of a washer or a window in a frame. Off: only the size limit decides."],["Smallest hole","Holes narrower than this are left alone."],["Depth limit","How far the inside of a hole may reach, as a multiple of the size limit. Lower it to leave deep bores alone."],["Flatness","How far apart the directions of two triangles may be and still count as one flat face. Raise it for meshes whose flat faces are slightly bent."],["Reset to defaults","Puts every option back."]]],["h","Undo"],["p","A whole run is one undo step. Press [[Ctrl + Z]] and every hole is back."],["h","Tips"],["list",["Start with a small limit, look at the result, and raise it. A limit that is too high closes openings you wanted, such as a cable cut-out.","The filled result is what gets saved and exported.","A hole that was formed by pushing through the sheet, with a collar on the other side, is closed, and its collar stays as a small stub."]],["p","[Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) can run this command as one of its steps, with the settings and the size limit of this panel."],["see",["smart-fit","decimate","smart-optimise","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"split","group":"lighter","title":"Split","summary":"Take apart a mesh that arrived as one piece but is really several separate solids, so each can be selected on its own.","blocks":[["p","Some files arrive with several solid pieces fused into one part: a bracket, its pin and two bolts as a single row in the tree. You cannot hide, delete or simplify one of them without the others. Split finds the pieces that do not touch and makes each one a part of its own."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-split.webp","Split: recovers the separate solids of a mesh that was fused on export."],["h","When to use it"],["p","When clicking one thing selects several things that are clearly separate objects. An STL file, for example, holds a whole model as a single mesh."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the part that should come apart.","Press [[X]], or click {{Split}} on the bottom toolbar. The panel opens.","Read the preview line in the panel. It says what the split would produce.","Press {{Split selected}}, or [[Enter]].","The part is replaced by its pieces in the tree. A message says how many parts were made."]],["p","To check every part of the model at once, press {{Scan whole model}} instead."],["h","What the options mean"],["p","The four buttons at the top set how readily pieces are told apart:"],["terms",[["Strict","The most pieces. Nothing is joined. Use it if you suspect the file has doubled points along its edges."],["Tight","The default. Points that are exact duplicates are joined, which keeps one solid whole along its sharp edges without joining neighbouring solids."],["Normal","Also joins points that are a tiny distance apart, to allow for small rounding differences."],["Loose","Joins points within 0.1% of the model’s size, so solids that touch are joined on purpose. Use it when you want fewer, larger parts."]]],["p","{{Algorithm}} chooses how connection is decided:"],["terms",[["Vertex connectivity","The default. Triangles that share a corner point belong together."],["Edge connectivity","Stricter. Triangles belong together only when they share a whole edge, so solids that meet at a single point stay separate."],["Spatial proximity (AABB)","Groups triangles by where they are in space instead of how they are connected. Useful when the connections in the file are fused but the solids are still apart."],["Hybrid (vertex + spatial)","Vertex connectivity first, then a second pass by position on any piece that still holds more than half of the mesh."]]],["h","Undo"],["p","One split is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]] puts the part back together."],["note","If the message says no separable parts were found, the mesh is one connected solid at this setting. Try {{Strict}}, or the {{Spatial proximity (AABB)}} algorithm."],["see",["parts-tree","select-parts","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"smart-fit","group":"lighter","title":"Smart fit","summary":"Replace a detailed part with a simple stand-in of the same size and position: a box, a cylinder, a few boxes, or blocks that keep its outline.","blocks":[["p","Some parts only need to be there. A bolt can carry a great many triangles, and from a distance a plain cylinder looks the same. Smart fit replaces the selected parts with a simple shape of the same size, in the same place."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-smart-fit.webp","Smart fit: box, turned box, cylinder, a few boxes, or blocks at the detail you choose."],["h","When to use it"],["p","For nuts, bolts, pins and housings: parts that fill a space but whose detail does not matter. Not for parts whose shape people will look at. This is the most drastic of the reducing tools: the part’s own mesh is thrown away."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the parts to replace.","Press [[Ctrl + B]], or click {{Smart fit}} on the bottom toolbar. The panel opens.","Choose a shape, or leave it on {{Auto}}.","Press {{Smart fit}}, or [[Enter]].","Each selected part becomes its stand-in. Its size and position are kept."]],["h","The shapes"],["terms",[["Auto","Picks a box, a turned box or a cylinder for each part, whichever fits it best."],["Box","A box lined up with the scene’s axes."],["Turned box","A box turned to follow the part. Better for parts that sit at an angle."],["Cylinder","A cylinder along the part’s long axis."],["A few boxes","A handful of boxes, each sized and turned to one piece of the part. A levelling foot becomes a slab and a bar, a frame becomes four bars. Each box is 12 triangles."],["Blocks","The part rebuilt from a coarse grid of boxes. It looks boxy, but its steps, arms and openings stay where they are."]]],["p","One box says where a part is and nothing about what it is: a bracket becomes a brick. {{A few boxes}} and {{Blocks}} keep the outline. Both fall back to a plain box when they would not be clearly lighter than the part."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-blocks.webp","Smart fit with Blocks on the two heaviest parts of the derailleur: 172,894 triangles down to 114, the outline kept."],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["At most … boxes","For {{A few boxes}}: the most boxes a part may become. Simple parts use fewer. It starts at 6."],["Blocks detail","For {{Blocks}}: how many cells the part’s longest side is cut into. More cells follow the outline more closely and cost more triangles. An opening narrower than a cell closes. It starts at 8."],["Roundness for a cylinder","Under {{Advanced}}. How round a part must be, looking down its axis, to become a cylinder. 1 is a perfect circle, a hexagon is 0.87, a square 0.71."],["Cylinder to box volume","A cylinder is chosen only if its volume is at most this share of the best box’s volume."],["Shortest cylinder, length to width","How many times longer than it is wide a part must be to become a cylinder. 0 lets flat round parts such as gears and washers be cylinders too."],["Find the part's own axes","Works out which way each part points from its mesh. Off: only the scene’s axes and the axes stored with the part are tried."]]],["h","Every part at once"],["p","The button {{Smart fit all parts}} in the card {{Reduce triangles}} replaces every part in the scene with a box, a turned box or a cylinder. It removes most of the detail of the model. Use it only when you need the roughest possible stand-in for a whole assembly."],["h","Undo"],["p","One run is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]] brings the original meshes back."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["Only the selected parts change. Another part with the same shape that stands elsewhere is left as it is. Only a copy that lies on the selected part itself, drawn twice in the same place, is fitted along with it.","If the view is exploded, Smart fit works on the parts at rest and then puts the explosion back as it was."]],["note","A quick way to simplify all fasteners: press {{Select fasteners}} in [Fasteners](#fasteners), then run Smart fit with {{Cylinder}}. For the copies of one fastener only, select one, press {{Select similar}} in the card {{Selection & actions}}, then run Smart fit with {{Cylinder}}."],["see",["decimate","fasteners","select-parts","smart-optimise","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"decimate","group":"lighter","title":"Decimate","summary":"Remove a share of the triangles from the selected parts while keeping their shape. Choose a percentage or a target, and preview first.","blocks":[["p","Decimate thins out the triangles of a part while keeping its overall shape. It is the tool for a part that matters and is simply too dense."],["p","It is lossy: the removed detail is gone from the mesh. It can be undone."],["h","When to use it"],["p","On the heavy parts you found in [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers), after you have deleted what is not needed. For a whole scene at once, [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget) is easier."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the parts to reduce.","Open the card {{Reduce triangles}} in the right sidebar, under Reduce.","In the list beside {{Decimate}}, choose how much to remove: −25%, −50%, −75% or −90%.","Press {{Preview…}} under it. A table shows each part with its triangle count now and after.","Press {{Decimate}}. The button shows how many parts are selected.","Look at the parts. If they have lost too much, press [[Ctrl + Z]] and try a smaller percentage."]],["h","A triangle target instead of a percentage"],["steps",["Choose {{Target…}} in the list.","Type the number of triangles that everything selected may have together.","A line under the field says what will happen, in the form “12,400 → about 6,200 triangles”.","Press {{Decimate}}. Each selected part gives up the same share."]],["h","What it keeps"],["list",["Normals, so the shading stays smooth.","UVs, the coordinates that place a texture on a surface.","Vertex colours.","Material ranges, for a part that uses more than one material."]],["h","Good to know"],["list",["It runs in the background, several parts at a time. The window stays usable, and a long run can be stopped.","The reduced mesh is what gets saved and exported.","Decimate has no keyboard shortcut on purpose, because it is lossy. Find it in the card, in the `…` list or with the search."]],["note","A common mistake is to decimate everything by 90% at once. Curved parts become visibly faceted. Reduce in steps, and reduce the densest parts more than the rest."],["see",["fit-to-budget","heavy-parts","read-the-numbers","smart-fit","smart-optimise"]]]},{"slug":"fit-to-budget","group":"lighter","title":"Fit to budget","summary":"Type the number of triangles the whole scene may have. The densest meshes are reduced first until the total fits.","blocks":[["p","Often you are given one number: “the model must be under 500,000 triangles”. Fit to budget takes that number and decides for you which parts to reduce and by how much. The densest meshes give up the most. Large, sparse ones are left alone."],["h","When to use it"],["p","Near the end, after you have deleted the parts that are not needed. Deleting is free. Reducing costs detail, so reduce only what is left."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Reduce triangles}} in the right sidebar.","Type the budget in the field beside {{Fit to budget}}. You can write `150000`, `150k` or `1.5m`.","Press {{Preview…}}. A table lists the meshes that will be reduced, with their triangle count now and after, and an estimate of the new total.","Press {{Fit to budget}}.","A progress card appears. {{Cancel}} stops it, and then nothing is changed.","A message gives the result, for example how many triangles the scene went from and to, and how many meshes were reduced."]],["h","What to expect"],["list",["The preview is an estimate. A mesh can stop a little short of its share.","Every mesh keeps at least 16 triangles. If the budget is lower than the scene can reach, the app goes as far as it can and says that the budget was not reached.","If the scene is already under the budget, the app says so and changes nothing."]],["h","Undo"],["p","The whole step is one undo: [[Ctrl + Z]] restores every mesh."],["note","If the result looks too rough, undo, delete more of what is not needed (see [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts) and [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts)), and run it again. The same budget then leaves more triangles for the parts that remain."],["p","[Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) can do this as its last step, together with the other clean-ups. The whole run is then one undo step. Its card is just above the Reduce triangles card in the sidebar."],["see",["decimate","smart-optimise","heavy-parts","read-the-numbers","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"cleanup","group":"lighter","title":"Auto cleanup and Tidy geometry","summary":"Small one-click repairs: remove empty parts and empty groups, fix shading, recentre the model and make moves permanent.","blocks":[["p","Two cards in the right sidebar, under Clean up, hold one-click commands. Each is one undo step."],["h","Auto cleanup"],["terms",[["Remove empty parts","Deletes parts that contain no triangles. Files often carry a few of these."],["Fix degenerate parts","Removes parts whose triangles have no area, which can confuse the programs that draw them."],["Delete empty groups","Removes groups that have no parts left in them."],["Deduplicate geometry","Looks for parts whose mesh data is identical to another part’s. See the note below before you use it."]]],["p","Each command reports what it did, or says that it found nothing."],["note","Be careful with {{Deduplicate geometry}} on an assembly. It removes the repeats of a part wherever they stand and keeps the first one, so a set of identical bolts is reduced to a single bolt. Check the parts count afterwards, and press [[Ctrl + Z]] if it took parts you wanted. To remove only copies that sit in the same place, use [Stacked copies](#stacked-copies) instead."],["p","[Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) starts every run with the first three commands, empty parts, degenerate parts and empty groups, under the name {{Remove empty and broken parts}}. It does not run {{Deduplicate geometry}} or any of the Tidy geometry commands."],["h","Tidy geometry"],["terms",[["Recompute smooth normals","Works out the shading directions of every surface again. Use it when parts look oddly faceted or dark after large changes."],["Recentre on origin","Moves the model so the centre of its bounding box sits at 0,0,0."],["Bake gizmo transforms","Makes the moves, turns and scalings you did with the gizmo permanent in the mesh itself."],["Centre pivot","Moves the pivot of each selected part to the centre of its bounding box. The part does not move on screen."]]],["h","Flags"],["p","The card {{Filters & flags}}, under Inspect, marks suspicious parts without changing them:"],["terms",[["Flag low-triangle parts","Marks parts with fewer triangles than the slider says."],["Flag thin slivers","Marks parts that are extremely long for their width, such as gaskets, wires and decals."]]],["p","The status bar shows how many parts are flagged. Click that number to isolate them, look at them, and decide what to do."],["see",["stacked-copies","smart-optimise","parts-tree","align-to-floor","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"workflow","group":"lighter","title":"A sensible order to work in","summary":"A suggested routine for a first clean-up: look, delete what is not needed, simplify what remains, check, save and export.","blocks":[["p","This is a suggestion, not a rule. It follows from what the tools do: deleting a part costs nothing in quality, so delete first. Reducing a mesh costs detail, so reduce only what you decided to keep. And there is no point in simplifying a part you are about to delete."],["p","If you want the shortest way, [Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) follows this same order for you: one button, one undo. The steps below are for when you want to look at the result of each tool before the next one."],["steps",["Open the file and look at it. Read the totals in {{Properties}}. See [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers).","If the model lies on its side or sits far from the grid, stand it up with [Align to floor](#align-to-floor).","Find out where the weight is: open [Heavy parts](#heavy-parts) and press [[4]] for the heatmap. Delete the heavy parts that nobody needs.","Remove what nobody will see with [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts). Isolate, check, delete.","Remove copies that lie on top of each other with [Stacked copies](#stacked-copies). Find, isolate, check, delete.","Find the bolts, nuts and washers with [Fasteners](#fasteners). Delete them if the model does not need them, or replace them with stand-ins using [Smart fit](#smart-fit).","Remove other small parts with [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts), if the model does not need them.","Close holes with [Fill holes](#fill-holes).","Replace other filler parts with stand-ins, using [Smart fit](#smart-fit).","Reduce the heavy parts that remain with [Decimate](#decimate), or bring the whole scene to a number with [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget).","Tidy up: {{Remove empty parts}} and {{Delete empty groups}} in the card {{Auto cleanup}}.","Look at the whole model again in the normal view ([[1]]) and in clay ([[5]]). Open the {{Optimisation report}} to see what you saved.","Save the scene, so you can come back to it. See [Save a scene](#save-scene).","Export the file for the program it is meant for. See [Export](#export)."]],["h","Habits that help"],["list",["Check before you delete. {{Isolate}} shows exactly what a tool has selected.","Work in steps and look after each one. [[Ctrl + Z]] takes back any of them.","Save a scene before a drastic step. Your original file is never touched, and a saved scene is a second safety net.","Stop when the number is reached. A model that is lighter than it needs to be has lost detail for nothing."]],["see",["smart-optimise","read-the-numbers","save-scene","export"]]]},{"slug":"view-modes","group":"look","title":"View modes","summary":"Six ways to draw the same model: solid, wireframe, X-ray, heatmap, clay and CAD. One key each, plus the Shading card for the clay and CAD looks.","blocks":[["p","A view mode changes how the model is drawn, not the model itself. Switch with the number keys. The first four also have buttons on the tool strip at the left edge of the viewport. Clay and CAD are chosen on the {{Shading}} card in the right sidebar (see below)."],["keys",[["1","Solid: the normal view, with colours and lighting"],["2","Wireframe: only the edges of the triangles"],["3","X-ray: every part as a see-through shell"],["4","Heatmap: parts coloured by triangle density"],["5","Clay: every part in one plain material. Press it again to return to solid."],["6","CAD: flat, matte shading with a dark line on every sharp edge. Press it again to return to solid."]]],["h","Solid"],["p","The everyday view. Parts have their own colours and are lit by a soft studio and a light that follows the camera, so dark parts keep an edge and a sheen."],["h","Wireframe"],["p","Shows the triangles themselves. Use it to see how dense a mesh is, and to check what Decimate has done. Where the lines are so close that they merge into a solid patch, the mesh is very dense."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-wireframe.webp","Wireframe: every edge of every part."],["h","X-ray"],["p","Makes every part translucent, so you can see what is inside without hiding anything."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-xray.webp","X-ray: the whole assembly as translucent shells."],["h","Heatmap"],["p","Colours each part by how many triangles it has for its size. It answers the question “where is the weight?” at a glance. See [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers)."],["h","Clay"],["p","Takes the colours away. Every part gets the same plain material, so all that is left is form: edges, curvature, and what stands in front of what. It is a good view for judging whether a simplified model still reads as the same object."],["p","Clay has four looks: {{Clay}} (matte grey), {{Porcelain}} (pale and glossy), {{Steel}} (metal) and {{Red wax}}. You choose the look on the Shading card."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-clay.webp","Clay: every part in one plain material, so only the form is left."],["h","CAD"],["p","The look of a CAD program. There are no lights, no reflections and no shadows. A surface gets its shade only from the way it faces the camera: lighter where it looks left, darker where it looks right, and a warm sand tone where it looks up. (This kind of shading is called a matcap: a picture of one lit ball, and every surface takes the colour of the spot on the ball that faces the same way.)"],["p","A grey or white part takes the shading as it is. A coloured part keeps its colour, with the same shading on top. A fine dark line follows the sharp edges of every part, and the sides of round parts get a thin dark outline too, so the form stays easy to read."],["p","CAD has three looks: {{Ceramic}} (the default, with warm tops), {{Light}} (brighter) and {{Mono}} (plain grey, no warm tone)."],["h","The Shading card"],["p","In the right sidebar, under {{Properties}}, is the {{Shading}} card. If it is folded, click its header to open it. It is one row of small 3D nuts, each drawn in its own look, so you see what a look does before you choose it. The row scrolls sideways when the sidebar is narrow."],["terms",[["Default","The plain Solid view, with the model’s own materials."],["Clay, Porcelain, Steel, Red wax","The four clay looks."],["Ceramic, Light, Mono","The three CAD looks. They carry a small CAD badge."],["Outlines","A switch under the row. It turns the dark lines on sharp edges on or off."]]],["steps",["Click a look. The view switches to clay or CAD, whichever the look belongs to, and the look you clicked is lit.","Click another look to change it. The model changes at once.","Click the lit look again to go back to Solid. Clicking {{Default}} does the same."]],["p","Keys 5 and 6 do the same as clicking: they switch to clay or CAD with the last look you used, and press again to go back to solid."],["h","Outlines"],["p","The {{Outlines}} switch draws a fine dark line along the sharp edges of every part. It works in Solid, Clay and CAD views, and is on to begin with. It is not drawn in Wireframe, X-ray or Heatmap. The same switch is under {{Settings}}, {{Viewport}}, together with the choice of CAD look."],["p","On a very large scene, the smallest parts may be drawn without outlines to keep the view quick. The app tells you when it does this."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["A view mode never reaches a file. Saving and exporting switch to solid while they run, then switch back.","To see inside a model, use X-ray, [Hide, isolate, show all](#hide-isolate) or [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts).","The background and the lighting are under {{Settings}}, {{Viewport}} and {{Settings}}, {{Scene}}. CAD does not use the lights at all.","A [screenshot](#screenshots) uses the view that is on, unless you choose Solid, Wireframe or X-ray in the screenshot window."]],["see",["read-the-numbers","hide-isolate","screenshots"]]]},{"slug":"colours-materials","group":"look","title":"Colours and materials","summary":"Open the materials dock to see every material in the scene, recolour all the parts that share one, and assign materials to parts.","blocks":[["p","A material is what a surface looks like: its colour, and how rough or metallic it is. Parts share materials. A CAD file with fifty grey brackets usually has one grey material that all fifty use. Change that material and all fifty change."],["h","Open the materials dock"],["p","Press [[Shift + M]], or click the palette button on the bottom toolbar. The dock slides up from the bottom of the window and shows every material as a small ball, with the number of parts that use it."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-materials.webp","The materials dock: every material in the scene, with how many parts use it."],["h","Find a material"],["list",["Type in {{Filter…}} to filter by name, by colour (as a hex code such as `#ff6600`) or by type.","Choose the order: {{Most used}}, {{Most triangles}}, {{Name}} or {{Colour}}.","Switch between a grid and a list with the two small buttons.","Select a part in the viewport and look at {{Properties}}: it shows the part’s material."]],["h","See where a material is used"],["steps",["Click a material in the dock.","The inspector at the right of the dock shows its colour, roughness, metalness and opacity, and how many parts and triangles use it.","Press {{Select parts}} to select every part that uses it, or {{Isolate}} to see only those parts."]],["h","Recolour"],["steps",["Double-click a material in the dock, or select it and press {{Edit}} in the inspector.","Change the colour in the editor that opens. You can also change how rough or metallic the surface is.","Every part that shares the material changes at once."]],["terms",[["Roughness","How matt the surface is. Low is glossy, high is dull."],["Metalness","How much the surface behaves like metal."],["Opacity","How solid the surface is. Lower values are see-through."]]],["h","Give parts a different material"],["steps",["Select the parts in the viewport or in the tree.","Click the material you want in the dock.","Press {{Assign}} in the inspector."]],["h","Make, copy, merge, delete"],["p","The buttons at the right of the dock’s header:"],["terms",[["New material","Makes a new material. If parts are selected, they get it."],["Duplicate","Copies the picked material."],["Merge","Turns several picked materials into one. Hold [[Ctrl]] and click to pick several."],["Delete","Deletes the picked materials."],["Industry presets","Applies a ready-made look to the picked material, such as brushed steel, glass or rubber."]]],["p","A right-click on a material gives the same commands, and one to copy its colour code."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["A new material is named after its colour, and the name follows the colour when you change it.","Every change in the dock is an undo step.","Drag the top edge of the dock to make it taller. Double-click the edge to reset it.","The dock and the console share the same place and take turns."]],["see",["view-modes","select-parts","export"]]]},{"slug":"move-rotate-scale","group":"look","title":"Move, rotate, scale and measure","summary":"Use the gizmo to move, turn and resize parts, drag a part over other surfaces with Dynamic place, type exact values in the transform card, and measure the distance between two points.","blocks":[["p","Select a part and a gizmo appears on it: a set of coloured handles. Red is the X axis, green is Y and blue is Z. Drag a handle to change the part along that axis."],["keys",[["E","Move"],["R","Rotate"],["T","Scale"],["Q","Hide the gizmo"],["D","Dynamic place: drag the selected part over other surfaces"],["Shift","Hold while dragging to snap to round steps"]]],["h","Move a part"],["steps",["Select the part. To move several parts together, select them all, or group them first with [[Ctrl + G]].","Press [[E]].","Drag one of the arrows. A small read-out shows how far the part has moved.","Hold [[Shift]] while you drag to move in round steps sized to the model."]],["p","Rotate ([[R]]) and scale ([[T]]) work the same way. With [[Shift]] held, rotation snaps to 15° and scale to steps of 0.1."],["h","Choose the snap step"],["p","While you hold [[Shift]] and drag a handle, turn the mouse wheel to change the step. Wheel up gives a coarser step and wheel down a finer one. A move steps through 1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50 and so on. A turn steps through 1°, 5°, 10°, 15°, 30°, 45° and 90°. A scale steps from 0.01 to 1. The part takes the new step at once, and the step is kept for your next drags."],["p","While snap is on, a small green pill at the top of the viewport, beside the camera pill, shows the step, for example {{Snap 10 mm}} or {{Snap 15°}}."],["h","Type exact values"],["steps",["Click the transform panel button on the viewport’s tool strip, the fifth button from the top. The {{Transform}} card rises into place at the foot of the left sidebar, under the parts tree.","It has three columns, {{Position}}, {{Rotation}} and {{Size}}, with a row for X, Y and Z.","Click a number and type a value, or drag it sideways.","Press {{Reset}} to put position, rotation and size back."]],["p","Right-click a column heading to copy all three values or paste them onto another part. The units follow {{Display units}} under {{Settings}}, {{Scene}}."],["h","Dynamic place"],["p","Dynamic place moves a part by dragging it over the model, instead of by its handles. The part rests on whatever surface is under the pointer and turns to face it, as when you drag a part out of the [library](#library) onto a model. It is handy for putting a bolt on a plate or a bracket on a wall."],["steps",["Select the part, or several parts.","Press [[D]], or click the fourth button on the tool strip (an arrow pointing down at a dot). The {{Dynamic place}} card opens over the viewport. The gizmo is hidden, and a thin blue box appears round the selection to show which box is being set down.","Press on the selected part and drag. The part follows the pointer and rests on the surface under it. The underside of the box touches the surface and never sinks into it.","Let go to drop it there.","Press [[Esc]] or {{Done}} to close the card and get the gizmo back."]],["p","A press that is not on the selected part selects as usual, and a press with [[Shift]], [[Ctrl]] or [[Alt]] held is left alone. If the view is exploded, put the parts back together first."],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Align to surface","On to begin with. The part turns so that its top points away from the surface it rests on. Off: the part keeps the way it faces and only slides."],["Land on the grid","On to begin with. Where nothing is under the pointer, the part lands on the grid. Off: it stays where it last rested."],["Top axis","Which of the part’s own axes, X, Y or Z, counts as its top. It is the part’s axes as it stands when you first pick it up. Z is the default when Z is up."],["Direction","{{+}} or {{−}}: whether the top axis points away from the surface or into it. Choose {{−}} to put a part on upside down."],["Gap","How far the part floats above the surface, in millimetres. A negative number sinks it in."],["Turn","Turns the part about the surface it rests on. Type an angle, or press 0°, 90°, 180° or 270°."],["Reset gap and turn","Sets the gap and the turn back to 0."]]],["p","While you hold a part, the mouse wheel turns it in steps of 15°, and the {{Turn}} box follows. Press [[Esc]] during a drag and the part goes back to where it was before the drag."],["p","The whole drag is one undo step. Choosing a gizmo with [[E]], [[R]] or [[T]] also ends Dynamic place and closes the card."],["h","Copy and duplicate"],["keys",[["Ctrl + D","Duplicate the selection"],["Ctrl + C","Copy the selection"],["Ctrl + V","Paste"]]],["h","Lock a part"],["p","Right-click a part in the tree and choose {{Lock}}. A locked part cannot be moved, and delete skips it. Choose {{Unlock}} in the same menu to release it."],["h","Measure"],["steps",["Press [[M]], or click the ruler on the bottom toolbar.","Click a point on the model. A dot follows the pointer to show where you will pick.","Click a second point. The distance appears between them.","Measure again, or press [[M]] or [[Esc]] to leave measure mode."]],["list",["Dragging still turns the view while you measure.","While you measure, a yellow {{Measure}} pill sits at the top of the viewport. Click it to leave measure mode.","Measurements stay on screen after you leave measure mode, and are listed in the card {{Measurements}} in the right sidebar. Click a row there to pick that measurement: its line gets thicker and its label turns yellow.","{{Clear all}} in that card removes them."]],["h","Undo"],["p","Moves, turns and scalings are undo steps, whether you dragged the gizmo or typed a value."],["note","Moving a part with the gizmo changes where it sits, not its mesh. To write the change into the mesh itself, use {{Bake gizmo transforms}} in the card {{Tidy geometry}}. Most people never need to."],["see",["align-to-floor","select-parts","cleanup"]]]},{"slug":"align-to-floor","group":"look","title":"Align to floor","summary":"Stand a model upright on the grid, centred over the origin, when it arrived on its side or far away from the centre of the scene. A small drawing shows the result first.","blocks":[["p","Files arrive anywhere and any way up: a part modelled 40 metres from its origin, an assembly lying on its side. Align to floor turns the whole model in steps and puts it on the grid, over the point 0,0,0."],["p","It is a floating card over the viewport. A small 3D drawing at the top shows what will happen. The model itself does not move until you press the {{Align to floor}} button."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-align.webp","Align to floor: turn the model in steps and choose which side of it sits at zero."],["h","When to use it"],["p","Right after opening a file, before anything else, if the model is not standing the way you expect. A model that sits on the floor at the origin is easier to orbit, and easier to place in the next program."],["h","Open the card"],["list",["Press [[Ctrl + K]] and choose {{Align model to the floor…}}.","Or click the three dots at the right end of the bottom toolbar (All commands) and choose {{Align to the floor…}}.","Press [[Esc]] or {{Done}} to put the card away."]],["h","The drawing"],["p","The drawing shows the floor with its grid, the origin where the three axes meet, and the model as the box it fills. The model’s own top and front are two arrows on it. They turn with the model, so you can see which way it will end up. A yellow ring marks the point of the model that will go to zero, and a dashed line joins it to the origin while the two are apart."],["p","While the drawing differs from the real model, it says {{Preview: not applied yet}}. With no model open, it draws an example box, so you can try the buttons before you open a file."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card.","If the model lies on its side, use the turn buttons. Each of the rows X, Y and Z has two arrows that turn the model about that axis, one step back or one step forward. A step is 90° to begin with.","Press them until the arrows in the drawing show the model the right way up. Only the drawing turns.","Choose which side of the model goes to zero with the buttons {{Low}}, {{Mid}}, {{High}} and {{Keep}}. Again only the drawing changes.","Press {{Align to floor}}. Now the model turns and moves so that it stands on the grid with its middle over 0,0,0. You watch it move."]],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Turn, step","How far one press turns the model. 90 gives quarter turns. A small number straightens a model that is slightly tilted."],["Sits at zero with its: X and Y","Which side of the model goes to 0 on that axis: {{Low}}, {{Mid}} (the middle), {{High}}, or {{Keep}} to leave it where it is. The rows are named after the scene’s floor axes: X and Y when Z is up."],["Sits at zero with its: Up","{{Floor}} stands the model on the grid. {{Top}} hangs it from the grid. {{Mid}} centres it. {{Keep}} leaves its height alone."],["Align to floor","Does it: turns and moves the model as the drawing shows."],["Reset","Puts the model back where and how it was loaded, and puts the card back to its start: no turn waiting, and Mid, Mid and Floor."]]],["p","Mid, Mid and Floor is the usual choice: it stands the model on the grid, centred over the origin."],["p","To repeat the last choices without opening the card, press [[Ctrl + K]] and choose {{Align model to the floor (with the last choices)}}."],["h","Undo"],["p","Pressing {{Align to floor}} is one undo step. {{Reset}} in the card goes back to how the file was loaded."],["note","Which direction is “up” is a setting of the scene: {{Up axis (viewport)}} under {{Settings}}, {{Scene}}. CAD files normally use Z as up. If the whole scene looks turned by a quarter, check that setting before you turn the model."],["see",["move-rotate-scale","export","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"exploded-view","group":"look","title":"Exploded view","summary":"Push the parts of an assembly apart with one slider, choose the directions, and put them back with one click.","blocks":[["p","An exploded view moves every part away from the centre of the model, so parts that sit inside or behind others come into the open. It is a way of looking, like in an assembly manual."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-exploded.webp","Exploded view, with a slider for each axis."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Exploded view}} in the right sidebar, under Inspect. If it is folded, click its header.","Drag the {{Amount}} slider. The parts move apart. The value is a percentage, and the slider goes up to 300%. You can click the number and type one.","Under {{Along}}, the buttons X, Y and Z say which directions the parts move in. All three are on to begin with. Switch one off and the parts stop moving along that axis. Switch it on again and they follow.","Under {{Quick}}, press 25%, 50%, 100% or 200% to jump to that amount.","Press {{Reset}} to bring every part back."]],["h","Fine tune each axis"],["p","Click {{Fine tune each axis}} to open three more sliders, {{X axis}}, {{Y axis}} and {{Z axis}}. Use them when you want a different amount in each direction, for example to pull a stack apart upwards a long way and sideways only a little. The {{Amount}} slider then follows the largest of the three."],["h","While the view is exploded"],["list",["A pill that says {{Exploded}}, with the amount, appears at the top of the viewport beside the camera pill. Click it to put the parts back and set the sliders to zero.","Moving a slider lets go of the selection. You can still click parts and see their outlines, but they have no gizmo to move them until the parts are back together.","Smart fit and Fill holes work on the model at rest, then put the explosion back as it was."]],["note","A suggestion: put the parts back before you edit, save or export. The exploded view is for looking, and a model is easier to work on when its parts are where they belong."],["see",["hide-isolate","view-modes","screenshots"]]]},{"slug":"library","group":"look","title":"The parts library and shapes","summary":"Add standard parts to a scene from a drawer under the viewport: 86 bolts, nuts, pipes, profiles and machine parts. Look at a part and set its size before it goes in.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser can also add parts. The library holds 86 ready-made parts. Each one is parametric, which means it is built from numbers such as a diameter and a length, and you can change those numbers before you place it and after."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-library.webp","The library drawer: a pillow block and a handwheel placed, and a stepper motor on its way in, shown where it will stand."],["h","Open the library"],["p","Press [[Shift + L]], or click the button with the small shapes on the bottom toolbar, just left of the palette button. The library is a drawer: it slides up from the bottom of the viewport and leaves the parts tree where it is. Press [[Shift + L]] again, or click the cross in its header, to close it."],["list",["Drag the top edge of the drawer to make it taller. Double-click the edge to reset it.","One drawer is open at a time. The library, the materials dock and the console give way to each other."]],["p","The drawer has two halves. On the left are the parts, in shelves, each with a small picture. On the right is the part you have picked: a view of it, its facts, its parameters, and the button that adds it."],["h","What is in it"],["terms",[["Shapes","Cube, sphere, cylinder, cone, torus, capsule, wedge, rounded box, star and more."],["Fasteners","Hex bolt, Allen screw, countersunk and button head screws, carriage bolt, U-bolt, eye bolt, set screw, threaded rod, rivet, washers, retaining ring, standoff."],["Nuts","Hex, lock, jam, flange, cap, castle, wing, thumb, square, coupling, eye and T-slot nuts."],["Pipes and flanges","Tube, elbow, tee, cross, reducer, pipe cap, flange."],["Profiles and plates","I-beam, box tube, angle, C-channel, T and Z profiles, T-slot extrusion, flat bar, round and hex bar, perforated and slotted plate, gusset, hinge."],["Machine parts","Gear, rack, sprocket, pulley, bearing, bushing, pillow block, shaft collar, linear rail, spring, handwheel, star knob, pull handle, levelling foot, wheel, enclosure, stepper motor."]]],["h","Find a part"],["list",["{{Search…}} in the header filters all the shelves as you type, by name or by shelf.","The dropdown next to it shows one shelf at a time. {{All shelves}} shows everything.","The parts you used last wait on a shelf called {{Recently used}} at the top. Its {{Clear}} button forgets them.","Pick a part and press the star in the right half to keep it on the {{Favourites}} shelf. Press the star again to take it off.","Two buttons in the header switch between large and small tiles."]],["h","Look at a part first"],["steps",["Click a tile. A single click only picks the part. It does not add it.","The right half shows the part. Drag it to turn it about any axis, so you can roll it over and see it from underneath. Use the mouse wheel to zoom. Double-click to go back to the first view.","Under the name are its number of triangles and its size.","Below that are the same rows as the {{Shape parameters}} of a placed part (see below). Change a number there and the view follows."]],["p","What you set in the right half is what the part has when it goes in. The numbers you change stay until you close the app’s page."],["h","Add a part"],["steps",["For a fastener, first choose its thread size and length in the drawer’s header, under {{Fasteners}}. M6 and 24 mm to begin with. Every screw, nut and washer you add comes out in that size, even one you changed in the right half.","Add the part in one of three ways: double-click its tile, or press {{Add to scene}} in the right half, or drag the tile into the viewport.","A double-click or {{Add to scene}} puts the part on the selected part, or on the floor at the origin when nothing is selected.","When you drag, a see-through copy follows the pointer and shows where the part will stand: square to the face under the pointer, or upright on the floor. It has the parameters you set. Let go to drop it.","The part is there, with a row of its own in the parts tree."]],["h","Change it afterwards"],["steps",["Select the part.","Look at {{Properties}} in the right sidebar. It now has a section called {{Shape parameters}}.","Drag a number sideways to change it, or click it and type. Hold [[Shift]] to go ten times faster and [[Alt]] to go ten times finer.","The part rebuilds as you go."]],["h","A bolt with its own nut"],["p","A hex bolt, an Allen screw, a threaded rod or an eye bolt can carry a nut."],["steps",["Select the bolt.","In {{Shape parameters}}, switch on {{Nut on the bolt}}.","Choose the kind in {{Nut}}: a hex nut, a wing nut, a castle nut and so on.","Set {{Nut from the head}}: how far from the head the nut sits."]],["p","The nut is part of the bolt’s own shape. It follows the bolt’s size and length, stays one row in the tree, and cannot sit past the end of the thread."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-bolt-nut.webp","A hex bolt that carries its own nut: size, length and the nut’s distance from the head are all parameters."],["h","Other ways to add a shape"],["list",["The search: press [[Ctrl + K]], type `add flange` and press [[Enter]].","The add button on the bottom toolbar, next to the search: a click adds the shape you used last, and holding the button down opens a choice of shapes.","The start screen: pick Cube, Sphere, Cylinder or Torus to start a new scene with that shape."]],["h","Undo"],["p","Adding a part is an undo step, and so is each change to its parameters."],["note","Library parts count like any other part: they have triangles, appear in the totals, and are saved and exported with the scene."],["see",["search-and-commands","move-rotate-scale","parts-tree"]]]},{"slug":"screenshots","group":"look","title":"Screenshots","summary":"Save a picture of the viewport as a PNG, at the size of the view or at any resolution up to poster size.","blocks":[["steps",["Set up the view: orbit to the angle you want and choose a view mode.","Click the camera button at the right end of the top bar. The screen flashes like a shutter and the {{Save screenshot}} window opens.","Choose a resolution.","Check the file name, or type your own.","Press {{Save…}}. Where the browser allows it, you choose the folder. Otherwise the picture goes to your downloads folder."]],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Resolution","Six presets: the viewport at 1×, 2× (sharp) or 4× (poster), and 1080p, 1440p and 4K."],["Custom (px)","Any width and height, from 16 to 16,384 pixels a side. The aspect ratio and the number of megapixels are shown beside it."],["View mode","{{Current}} uses the view mode that is on. {{Solid}}, {{Wireframe}} and {{X-ray}} use that mode for the picture only."],["Hide scene grid","Leaves the floor lines and the origin axes out of this picture."],["Filename","Starts as the model’s name and the time."],["Stamp info on image","Prints a small panel in a corner of the picture. Tick what it should show: model name, statistics, selection counts, timestamp, camera, units, and the app’s name. Then pick the corner."]]],["p","The picture is a PNG file. Right-clicking an empty spot of the viewport also offers {{Save screenshot…}}."],["note","A 4× picture of a large viewport is a very large file. The limit is 16,384 pixels a side."],["see",["view-modes","move-the-view","exploded-view"]]]},{"slug":"save-scene","group":"save","title":"Save a scene","summary":"Save your work as a scene file so you can carry on later. What a scene file is, how to name it with the file-name buttons, and how it differs from an export.","blocks":[["p","Saving a scene keeps your work so you can open it again and carry on. Exporting writes a file for another program. Do both: save a scene for yourself, export for whoever gets the result."],["h","What a scene file is"],["p","A scene file is a `.glb` file. It holds the model as it is now, after everything you deleted, reduced and recoloured. It also remembers things only MeshOptimiser uses: the camera, the view settings, which parts were hidden or flagged, and your measurements. Other programs can open the file as an ordinary GLB and ignore the rest."],["h","Save"],["steps",["Press [[Ctrl + S]]. You can also click the dot on the scene’s tab, or choose {{Menu}}, then {{Save scene…}}.","The {{Save scene}} window opens. At the top it says what is about to be written: how many parts, how many triangles and about how big the file will be.","Check the {{Filename}}, or type your own. The ending `.glb` is added for you. The buttons under the name can build it for you (see below).","Look at the options under the name, and press {{Save…}}.","Where the browser allows it, a file window opens. Choose a folder and confirm. Otherwise the file goes to your downloads folder.","The dot on the tab turns green."]],["p","Saving again under the same name writes to the same file without asking where. The window then says “Saves over” and the name of the file, and the button reads {{Save}}."],["h","Build the file name"],["p","Under the name are small buttons. Each one adds a piece to the name. Press it again and the piece comes off. The numbers are those of what is about to be written, and they follow the options below, so the name stays true."],["terms",[["+ date","Today’s date, like `2026-10-08`."],["+ time","The time now, like `1432h`."],["+ version","A version number like `v2`. Press it again to raise the number. Hold [[Shift]] while you press to take it off."],["+ triangles","How many triangles the file holds, like `17k-tris`."],["+ parts","How many parts, like `67-parts`."],["+ size","About how big the file is, like `370KB`."],["+ saving","How much lighter than the file you opened, like `-42pct`. It shows only when the new file is lighter."],["+ optimised","Adds the word `optimised`, to mark the file as the optimised one."],["+ selection","Adds the word `selection`, to mark the file as a part of the scene."],["Source name","Starts again from the name of the file you opened."]]],["p","A button that is in use shows a minus sign instead of the plus. You can also type in the name by hand."],["h","The options"],["terms",[["Whole scene","On: every part. Off: only the selected parts. It is dimmed when nothing is selected, and says how many parts are selected."],["Include hidden parts","Hidden parts are written too, and come back hidden when you open the scene again. It says how many are hidden."],["Remember the view and settings","Stores the camera, the display settings, flags and measurements in the file. Other programs ignore them."],["Save as a copy","Writes the file without making it this scene’s file. The scene keeps its unsaved mark, and the next save goes where it went before."]]],["p","A file that holds only some of the scene (only the selection, or no hidden parts) is never the scene’s own file. The app turns on {{Save as a copy}} for you."],["h","Know whether you have saved"],["p","The dot on the tab is yellow while there are changes that are not saved, and green when the scene is saved. Other tabs show a yellow dot too when they have unsaved changes. Closing a tab or quitting with unsaved changes asks first."],["h","Open a scene again"],["p","Open the `.glb` like any other file: drop it on the window, press [[Ctrl + O]], or pick it from the recent files on the start screen. The camera and the view settings come back with it, and hidden parts are still hidden."],["h","Scene or export?"],["terms",[["Save scene","Everything, including hidden parts, at the scene’s own scale and orientation. For continuing your work."],["Export","What you choose, in the format, scale and orientation the next program needs. For handing over."]]],["note","Your original file is never overwritten by a save, unless you choose its name and folder yourself. Give the scene a name of its own."],["see",["export","scene-tabs","undo-redo"]]]},{"slug":"export","group":"save","title":"Export","summary":"Write the model to GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL, PLY or a parts list, name the file, and set the scale, up axis and origin the next program needs.","blocks":[["h","Steps"],["steps",["Click {{Export}} at the right of the top bar, or press [[Ctrl + E]]. A list of formats drops down. The button is dimmed while no model is open.","Choose a format. The Export window opens with that format selected. You can still change it in the list on the left.","Check the file name. Set the options. For a first try, the defaults are fine.","Press the button at the bottom right, for example {{Export GLB}}.","The file arrives as a download in your browser, normally in your downloads folder. A message confirms it."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-export.webp","Export to GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL, PLY, or a parts list."],["h","The window"],["p","The list of formats is at the left, in groups: 3D formats, 3D printing, point cloud and data. The rest of the window is for the format you chose. From the top:"],["terms",[["Format and Compare","The name of the format and a line about it. {{Compare}} opens a sheet that shows what each format keeps."],["What will be written","Three figures that follow your options: how many parts, how many triangles and about how big the file will be. For a parts list: rows, columns and size."],["File name","The name the file gets, with the ending for the format. The buttons under it build the name (see below)."],["Size and position","Unit scale, up axis and origin."],["Parts","Which parts go in the file, and how they are arranged."],["Format options","Only the ones that fit the format."],["Bottom line","Says what the file will be called, for example “Saves as rd-fine.glb”. For OBJ it adds that a `.mtl` file with the colours comes with it. If no part matches your options, it says so and the export button is dimmed."]]],["p","The choices you make for scale, axis, origin and the parts switches are kept for the next export. The file name and {{Selected parts only}} are not."],["h","The formats"],["terms",[["GLB","Modern binary glTF, in one file. The app calls it best for the web and Cinema 4D."],["glTF","The same as GLB, written as text (JSON glTF 2.0)."],["FBX","Binary FBX 7.4. The app lists Blender, Maya, 3ds Max, Houdini and Unreal."],["USDZ","For Apple Quick Look and Reality Composer."],["OBJ","Wavefront OBJ. Universal, geometry only. The colours go in a `.mtl` file beside it."],["STL","Binary STL. The standard for 3D printing."],["PLY","Geometry with vertex colours."],["CSV","A parts list as a spreadsheet: name, colour, triangles, vertices, volume, size, and whether the part is flagged, visible or locked. No geometry is written."]]],["p","[Which format should I choose](#choose-format) helps you pick."],["h","Name the file"],["p","Type a name, or let the buttons under it build one. They are the same as in [Save a scene](#save-scene). Each button adds one piece to the name, and a second press takes it off. The counts and the size are those of what is about to be written, and they change when you change an option."],["terms",[["+ date, + time","Today’s date, or the time now."],["+ version","A version number. Press it again to raise it. Hold [[Shift]] to take it off."],["+ triangles, + parts, + size","The triangle count, the number of parts and the approximate file size."],["+ saving","How much lighter than the file you opened. It shows only when the export is lighter."],["+ optimised, + selection","The words `optimised` and `selection`."],["Source name","Starts again from the name of the file you opened."]]],["p","The ending is added for you, so leave it out. Characters a file name cannot have are replaced with `_`. When the name is empty, the scene’s own name is used."],["h","Size and position"],["terms",[["Unit scale","Multiplies every size in the file. {{1.0 (no change)}} keeps the model as it is. CAD models are usually in millimetres. If the next program works in metres, choose {{× 0.001 (mm → m)}}. The list also has cm to m, m to mm, m to cm, inches to mm, mm to inches, and {{Custom…}}."],["Custom scale","The multiplier, when {{Unit scale}} is on {{Custom…}}."],["Up axis","{{Keep as in the scene}} writes the model the way it stands. {{Turn Z-up into Y-up}} turns the model so Y is up, which is what glTF, OBJ and Maya expect. A model that already stands Y up, such as one that came from Cinema 4D or from a glTF, must be kept as it is. Turning it lays it on its side."],["Origin","{{Keep model origin}} leaves the model where it is. {{Recentre to bbox centre}} puts the middle of the model at 0,0,0. {{Centre XY, drop to floor (Z=0)}} centres it and stands it on the floor."]]],["p","The up axis and the scale you set in {{Settings}}, {{Scene}} only change how the scene is shown on screen. They do not go into the file. The two choices in this window decide what the file says."],["h","Parts"],["terms",[["Visible parts only","Leaves hidden parts out of the file. On by default."],["Selected parts only","Writes only the parts that are selected, shown or not. It overrides the switch above."],["Merge into one mesh","Writes the whole model as one object. Useful for 3D printing. The group tree is lost."],["Flatten groups","Writes every part at the top level, without the group folders. Handy for programs that stumble on groups inside groups. It is shown for the formats that have a tree (GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ), and it is dimmed when you merge into one mesh."]]],["h","Format options"],["terms",[["Draco compression","GLB only. Compresses the mesh data, which typically makes the file 5 to 20 times smaller. Exporting and loading are slower."],["ASCII FBX","FBX only. Writes the FBX as text instead of binary. Try it if the next program cannot read the binary file."],["ASCII STL","STL only. Writes the STL as readable text. It is much larger. Use it only for a program that cannot read binary STL."]]],["p","For a parts list, the options about scale, axis, origin and merging are left out, because no geometry is written."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["The export contains the model as it is now: deleted parts are gone and reduced meshes are reduced.","It runs in solid view and switches back afterwards, so a view mode never ends up in the file.","After an export, the {{Optimisation report}} shows the size of the exported file beside the size of the file you opened.","If the file you opened was compressed (Draco, Meshopt or quantised), the window says so, because an export without compression is two to twenty times larger.","Draco compression needs an internet connection the first time, because the app fetches its compression code on demand.","A very large model can be too big for a text format such as OBJ. The app then suggests GLB or STL, which are binary and much smaller.","An FBX says which unit it uses and which way is up, so the next program can read the size and the direction correctly. It also shares one material between all the parts of the same colour, which makes the file quicker to open.","OBJ keeps parts that share a name apart, by giving them a number: `name`, `name.001` and so on."]],["note","A common mistake is the wrong scale. If the model arrives a thousand times too big or too small in the next program, export again with a different {{Unit scale}}."],["note","Another is a model that arrives lying on its back. Export again with the other {{Up axis}}."],["see",["choose-format","save-scene","file-formats"]]]},{"slug":"choose-format","group":"save","title":"Which format should I choose","summary":"A short guide by purpose: the web, Cinema 4D, Blender, game engines, AR on an iPhone, 3D printing, or a parts list.","blocks":[["p","If you are not sure, export GLB. It is one file, it is compact, and it keeps the parts tree, the materials and the colours. The app’s own format sheet calls it the best all-round choice."],["terms",[["A web page or a web viewer","GLB. Switch on {{Draco compression}} only if the viewer you use can read it."],["Cinema 4D","GLB. The app recommends it for Cinema 4D 2026."],["Blender, Maya, 3ds Max, Houdini","FBX. These are the programs the app lists for it."],["Unreal","FBX. The app lists Unreal under FBX."],["Unity","The app does not name Unity for any format. FBX is the usual choice for game engines."],["AR on an iPhone or iPad","USDZ, the format of Apple Quick Look."],["3D printing","STL. Switch on {{Merge into one mesh}} if the printer software wants one object."],["A program that reads nothing else","OBJ. It is understood almost everywhere, but it carries no hierarchy: the tree is flattened into the names of the parts."],["Research or scanning tools","PLY, which holds geometry and a colour for every vertex."],["A list of parts for a spreadsheet","CSV."],["Carrying on later in MeshOptimiser","Not an export at all: [Save a scene](#save-scene)."]]],["h","What each format keeps"],["terms",[["GLB and glTF","The parts tree, full materials, vertex colours."],["FBX","The parts tree and materials. No vertex colours."],["USDZ","The parts tree and materials. No vertex colours."],["OBJ","Materials through a separate `.mtl` file. A flat list of parts, no tree."],["STL","Geometry only, as a single mesh. No colours."],["PLY","Geometry as a single mesh, with vertex colours."],["CSV","No geometry."]]],["h","Two settings that go with the format"],["list",["Scale. A CAD model is usually in millimetres. Many 3D programs and the glTF format work in metres. If the model arrives huge, export with {{× 0.001 (mm → m)}}.","Up axis. CAD uses Z as up. glTF, OBJ and Maya use Y. If the model arrives lying down, export with {{Turn Z-up into Y-up}}."]],["note","These are safe starting points. The program you are exporting to has the last word, so do one test export before you spend time on the model."],["see",["export","file-formats","save-scene"]]]},{"slug":"shortcuts","group":"reference","title":"Keyboard shortcuts","summary":"Every keyboard shortcut and mouse action in MeshOptimiser v0.14.0, grouped by what it does.","blocks":[["p","On macOS, use [[⌘]] wherever this page says [[Ctrl]]."],["p","Inside the app, press [[?]] for the same list with a search field. Shortcuts with a single key do nothing while you are typing in a field, or while a window is open in front."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-shortcuts.webp","The keyboard shortcuts overlay, with search."],["h","File"],["keys",[["Ctrl + N","New scene, in a new tab"],["Ctrl + O","Open a file"],["Ctrl + Shift + O","Import a file into the open scene"],["Ctrl + S","Save the scene"],["Ctrl + E","Export"],["Ctrl + Shift + F4","Close all scenes. It asks once about the ones with unsaved changes."],["Ctrl + ;","Scene settings"]]],["h","Edit"],["keys",[["Ctrl + Z","Undo"],["Ctrl + Y","Redo"],["Ctrl + Shift + Z","Redo"],["Ctrl + C","Copy the selected parts"],["Ctrl + V","Paste"],["Ctrl + D","Duplicate the selection"],["Del","Delete the selection. Backspace does the same."],["Ctrl + G","Group"],["Ctrl + Shift + G","Ungroup"],["Ctrl + M","Merge the selected parts into one"],["F2","Batch rename"]]],["h","Tools"],["keys",[["X","Split"],["P","Fill holes"],["Ctrl + B","Smart fit"],["M","Measure"],["Enter","Run the open command card"],["Esc","Put the open command card away. A second press clears the selection."]]],["p","Smart optimise, Decimate, Fit to budget, Select hidden parts, Fasteners, Stacked copies and Align to floor have no key of their own. Find them on the toolbar, in the `…` list or with the search."],["h","Selection"],["keys",[["Ctrl + A","Select all"],["Ctrl + I","Invert the selection"],["Esc","Select nothing"],["Shift + S","Find the selected part in the tree"]]],["h","Visibility"],["keys",[["H","Hide the selection"],["Shift + H","Hide the rest"],["Alt + H","Show all"],["S","Isolate the selection, and back"]]],["h","View"],["keys",[["F","Frame the selection, or the whole model"],["1","Solid"],["2","Wireframe"],["3","X-ray"],["4","Heatmap"],["5","Clay, and back to solid"],["6","CAD view, and back to solid"],["Ctrl + 1","Camera view (perspective)"],["Ctrl + 2","Top view"],["Ctrl + 3","Front view"],["Ctrl + 4","Side view"],["G","Show or hide the floor grid"]]],["h","Move, rotate, scale"],["keys",[["E","Move"],["R","Rotate"],["T","Scale"],["D","Dynamic place: drag the selected part over other surfaces. Press it again to leave."],["Q","Hide the gizmo"],["Shift","Hold while dragging the gizmo to snap"],["Shift + Scroll","While snapping and dragging: a coarser or a finer step"]]],["h","Panels and windows"],["keys",[["Ctrl + K","Search commands, parts and the library"],["Shift + L","Library drawer"],["Shift + M","Materials drawer"],["`","Console"],["Ctrl + ,","Settings"],["F1","Help window"],["?","Keyboard shortcuts"],["Ctrl + F","In Settings: jump to the search field"],["Ctrl + Enter","In Batch rename: apply"],["Esc","Close the open window or menu"]]],["h","In the help window"],["keys",[["/","Jump to the search field"],["Enter","In the search field: open the first result"],["Esc","Clear the search. Press it again to close the window."]]],["h","Mouse, in the viewport"],["keys",[["Click","Select a part"],["Shift + Click","Add to the selection"],["Ctrl + Click","Add to or remove from the selection"],["Ctrl + Drag","Box select"],["Ctrl + Shift + Drag","Box select, adding to the selection"],["Drag","Orbit"],["Middle drag","Pan"],["Right drag","Pan"],["Scroll","Zoom, toward the cursor"],["Alt + Right drag","Zoom smoothly"],["Right-click","Menu for the part or the view"]]],["h","Mouse, elsewhere"],["keys",[["Double-click","On a name in the tree or on a tab: rename"],["Click","In the tree: select a row"],["Shift + Click","In the tree: select a range of rows. In Heavy parts: tick a run of rows."],["Ctrl + Click","In the tree: add a row to the selection or take it off"],["Drag","In the tree: move rows into a group or into another place"],["Right-click","On a tab: duplicate, close all, close others, open"],["Middle click","On a tab: close it"],["Esc","While dragging a tab: put it back"],["Shift","While dragging a number: ten times faster"],["Alt","While dragging a number: ten times finer"],["Click","On a number: type a value"]]],["h","On the start screen"],["keys",[["← → ↑ ↓","Walk the cards of the recent files. Up and down move by a row."],["Enter","In the search field of the recent files: open the first match"]]],["see",["search-and-commands","move-the-view","select-parts","help-window"]]]},{"slug":"command-line","group":"reference","title":"The command-line converter","summary":"Convert STEP files to GLB without opening the viewer. How to run step2glb.py, and what every option does.","blocks":[["p","The converter is a Python script, `step2glb.py`. The app runs it for you when you drop a STEP file on the window. You can also run it yourself in a terminal, for example to convert many files, or to use options the import window does not show."],["p","You never need this page to use the app. It is here for when you want more control."],["h","Before you start"],["steps",["Start the app once with its launcher, so the Python environment exists. See [Start it the first time](#first-start).","Open a terminal in the app folder. On Windows: open the folder in File Explorer, click the address bar, type `cmd` and press [[Enter]]. On macOS: open Terminal, type `cd` and a space, drag the app folder onto the window, and press [[Enter]].","Switch on the app’s Python environment. This makes the command `python` use the add-ons the launcher installed. On Windows, type the first command below. On macOS, type the second."]],["code",".venv\\Scripts\\activate"],["code","source .venv/bin/activate"],["h","Convert a file"],["code","python step2glb.py input.step"],["p","Replace `input.step` with the path of your file. The result is written next to it, with the same name and the ending `.glb`. The terminal prints what the converter is doing."],["h","Examples"],["terms",[["`python step2glb.py input.step --quality 0.2 --min-size 0.5`","A finer tessellation, and parts smaller than 0.5% of the model left out."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --relative`","Quality as a fraction of the model’s size, so it does not depend on the file’s units."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --meshopt`","Compress the result with gltfpack."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --simplify 0.5`","Also halve the triangle count. Lossy."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --no-colors --no-instance`","The plain reader: no colours, no instancing."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --out small.glb --target-tris 200000`","Adjust the quality automatically until the result has at most 200,000 triangles."],["`python step2glb.py a.step b.step c.step`","Convert several files, one after the other."]]],["h","Input and output"],["terms",[["`input`","One or more STEP files."],["`--out`, `-o`","The file to write. Only with a single input file. Without it, the GLB is written next to the input."],["`--force`","Convert again even if an output that is newer than the input already exists. Without it, the converter skips a file that was already converted with the same settings."]]],["h","Mesh quality"],["terms",[["`--quality`, `-q`","How closely the triangles follow the true surface (the linear deflection). Smaller is finer and heavier. The default is 0.5, in the units of the file, normally millimetres."],["`--relative`","Reads `--quality` as a fraction of the model’s bounding-box diagonal instead of an absolute distance."],["`--angular`","The angular deflection, in radians: a second limit on how coarsely curved surfaces are cut into triangles. The default is 0.5, about 28.6°."],["`--min-size`","Leave out parts whose size is below this percentage of the model’s diagonal. The default, 0, keeps everything."]]],["h","Budgets"],["terms",[["`--target-tris`","Adjust `--quality` automatically to land at or under this triangle count. The conversion is run again up to five times."],["`--target-size-mb`","The same, for the size of the output file in megabytes. With both set, the tighter one wins."]]],["h","Repeated parts"],["terms",[["`--no-instance`","Do not collapse identical shapes into instances. According to the README, it only has an effect together with `--no-colors`."],["`--pca-instances`","An experimental, more aggressive way to find repeated shapes. It can turn symmetric parts the wrong way."]]],["h","Colours, names and the tree"],["terms",[["`--no-colors`","Use the plain reader: geometry only, no colours, names or hierarchy. The fastest."],["`--force-colors`","Always read colours, names and the hierarchy. Without this option, the converter skips them for files over 1 GB."],["`--no-shuo`","Skip per-instance colour overrides. A big saving in time on assemblies with many repeated parts."],["`--no-layers`","Skip CAD layer information."],["`--no-materials`","Skip material information."],["`--no-step-names`","Skip product names. Parts get generic names in the tree."],["`--no-step-props`","Skip validation properties such as mass and area."],["`--props`","Work out the volume and surface area of every solid. Slow on big files."]]],["h","Compression and reduction"],["p","These options use gltfpack, a separate free tool that has to be installed and on your PATH. Without it they are skipped, with a warning."],["terms",[["`--meshopt`","Compress the GLB with Meshopt, for files roughly ten times smaller. This is on by default whenever gltfpack is found."],["`--no-meshopt`","Switch that default off."],["`--quantize`","Store positions, normals and texture coordinates as smaller numbers."],["`--simplify`","Keep this fraction of the triangles, between 0 and 1. 0.5 halves the count. Sharp edges are preserved. Lossy. It switches Meshopt on."]]],["h","Speed"],["terms",[["`--parallel`","Use this many worker processes for building the meshes. The default, 0, does one at a time."]]],["h","Without typing"],["p","The folder also holds `step2glb.bat` (Windows) and `step2glb.command` (macOS). Drag a STEP file onto one of them to convert it with the default settings, without opening the viewer."],["h","Options of the server"],["p","The launcher starts `serve.py`. Started by hand, it takes these options:"],["terms",[["`--tab`","Open the app in an ordinary browser tab instead of a window of its own."],["`--no-browser`","Start the server and open nothing. Type the printed address into a browser yourself."],["`--open`, `-o`","Open this file at start. Accepts STEP, GLB and glTF files. For a STEP file it asks about quality first."],["`--port`","Use this port instead of 4242."]]],["see",["first-file","files-in-folder","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"file-formats","group":"reference","title":"File formats","summary":"Which files the app opens and which it writes, and what each format keeps: the parts tree, materials and vertex colours.","blocks":[["p","This page follows the format sheet inside the app. Open it there with {{Compare}} in the Export window."],["h","Formats it opens"],["terms",[["STEP (.step, .stp)","The CAD exchange format. Converted by the local Python converter. Keeps the parts tree and materials. Open only: the app does not write STEP."],["GLB","Binary glTF. Keeps the parts tree, full materials and vertex colours. The best all-round format."],["glTF","The text form of GLB. The same capabilities, larger."],["FBX","A common exchange format for Maya, Blender, Unreal and 3ds Max. Keeps the parts tree and materials."],["OBJ","Universal, but with no transforms and no tree. Materials come from a separate `.mtl` file."],["3MF","A 3D-printing format. Keeps the parts tree and materials. Open only."],["STL","Geometry only, as a single mesh. Used for 3D printing."]]],["p","STEP files go through the converter. The others load directly in the browser."],["h","Formats it writes"],["terms",[["GLB","Parts tree, full materials, vertex colours. Optional Draco compression."],["glTF","The same as GLB, as text."],["FBX","Parts tree and materials. Binary FBX 7.4 by default, with a text (ASCII) version as a fallback."],["USDZ","Parts tree and materials. For Apple Quick Look and Reality Composer. Write only."],["OBJ","A flat list of parts, with the hierarchy folded into their names. Materials in a `.mtl` file."],["STL","One mesh, geometry only."],["PLY","One mesh, with a colour for every vertex. Write only."],["CSV","A parts list. No geometry. Write only."]]],["h","The three columns of the sheet"],["terms",[["Hierarchy","Whether the format keeps the parts tree: groups, and groups inside groups."],["Materials","Colour and shading for each part. “PBR” means the full set with metalness and roughness. A plain dot means colour only."],["Vertex colours","A colour stored on every corner point of the mesh, with no separate material."]]],["see",["export","choose-format","first-file"]]]},{"slug":"files-in-folder","group":"reference","title":"Files in the folder","summary":"What the files and folders inside the MeshOptimiser folder are for, which ones you use, and which ones to leave alone.","blocks":[["p","You only ever need to double-click one file: the launcher for your system. The rest is the app itself."],["h","The ones you use"],["terms",[["`start.bat`","The launcher for Windows. Double-click it to start the app."],["`start.command`","The launcher for macOS."],["`start_hidden.vbs`","Windows: starts the app like `start.bat`, without showing the launcher window."],["`step2glb.bat`, `step2glb.command`","Convert a STEP file that you drag onto them, without opening the viewer."],["`test-converter.bat`, `test-converter.command`","Run the converter on a STEP file that you drag onto them and show everything it prints. Useful when a conversion fails and you want to see why."],["`README.txt`","A short quick start, in plain text."]]],["h","Folders the app creates"],["terms",[["`.venv`","The app’s own Python environment, created on the first run. It can take up to about 2 GB. It is safe to delete: the launcher builds it again, which takes a few minutes. On macOS the folder is hidden in Finder, because its name starts with a dot."],["`inbox`","Where STEP files are converted. The finished GLB files stay here."]]],["note","The `inbox` folder is not emptied automatically, so it grows with every conversion. With the app closed, you can delete the files in it that you no longer need."],["h","The app itself"],["terms",[["`serve.py`","The small local server. It hands the app’s pages to your browser and starts the converter when you drop a STEP file."],["`step2glb.py`","The converter from STEP to GLB."],["`index.html`, `app-v2.js`","The viewer: the page and its program."],["`holefill.js`","The hole filler behind Fill holes."],["`mesh-worker.js`","A background worker for Fill holes and Decimate, so the window stays usable while they run."],["`cloner.js`","A cloner that makes linear, grid and radial arrays of a part."],["`vendor`","The libraries the viewer needs, bundled so that it starts without the internet."],["`assets`","The app’s icons, pictures and help clips."],["`manifest.webmanifest`","What lets Chrome and Edge install the page as an app."],["`requirements.txt`","The list of Python add-ons the launcher installs: cadquery-ocp, trimesh and numpy."],["`tests`","The self-tests. See [Self-test](#self-test)."],["`docs`, `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`","Screenshots, the project’s description, and the list of changes in every version."]]],["note","Do not open `index.html` by double-clicking it. The page then says it needs a local server. Always start with the launcher."],["see",["first-start","command-line","stop-restart-update"]]]},{"slug":"privacy-offline","group":"reference","title":"Privacy and offline use","summary":"MeshOptimiser runs on your own computer. What stays local, when the internet is used, and what the browser remembers.","blocks":[["h","Your files stay on your computer"],["p","MeshOptimiser is not an online service. The viewer runs in your browser, and the converter runs in Python on your machine. The local server answers only on your own computer, at an address that starts with `localhost`."],["p","When you drop a STEP file, the page hands it to that local server, which converts it in the app’s `inbox` folder. The file does not go anywhere else. There is no account and no sign-in."],["h","When the internet is used"],["terms",[["The first start","The launcher downloads the Python add-ons the converter needs (cadquery-ocp, trimesh, numpy). If Python itself is missing and you agree, it downloads Python too."],["Every later start","Nothing. The libraries the viewer needs are bundled in the `vendor` folder, so the app opens, edits and exports with no connection at all."],["A few rarely used converters","Fetched from the internet the first time they are needed: the transcoder for compressed textures, and the converters behind Draco export and some older FBX files."]]],["h","What the browser remembers"],["p","The app keeps a few things in your browser’s storage, on this computer:"],["list",["your settings;","the list of recent files, with a small picture of each;","permission to reopen a recent file without asking where it is;","the sizes of the sidebars and docks."]],["p","You can clear each of these under {{Settings}}, {{Storage}}. Because they live in the browser’s profile, they survive an update of the app, and they are separate for each browser you use."],["h","What is written to disk"],["list",["Converted GLB files, in the `inbox` folder.","Scenes you save and files you export, where you choose.","The Python environment, in `.venv`."]],["see",["requirements","files-in-folder","settings"]]]},{"slug":"troubleshooting","group":"help","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Fixes for the usual problems: Python not found, a blocked launcher on macOS, missing modules, a blank view and slow conversions.","blocks":[["p","Find the heading that matches what you see. If nothing here helps, report the problem at `https://github.com/xpayn3/MeshOptimiser/issues`. Say which version you have, and paste the log: {{Copy log}} on the progress card, or the copy button in the console. In {{Settings}}, {{About}}, the button {{Copy details}} copies the version, your browser and your graphics card in one go."],["h","“python is not on PATH” on Windows"],["p","Windows cannot find Python. Run the Python installer again and tick “Add Python to PATH”. Or close and reopen the terminal or the launcher, so the new PATH is picked up. See [What you need](#requirements)."],["h","“Operation not permitted” on macOS"],["p","Right-click `start.command` and choose “Open”. macOS blocks a double-click on a freshly unzipped script the first time."],["h","“ModuleNotFoundError: No module named ‘OCP’”"],["p","The Python environment is incomplete. The same goes for a missing `numpy` or `trimesh`. Quit the app, delete the folder `.venv` inside the app folder, and start the launcher again. It rebuilds the environment from scratch."],["h","The first run stops while installing"],["list",["Check your internet connection. The first run has to download the add-ons.","Check your Python version. The converter needs 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12. With 3.13 the add-ons cannot be installed. Install a supported version, delete the folder `.venv` if one was created, and start again.","Check that the drive has about 2 GB free."]],["h","“This page needs a local server”"],["p","You opened `index.html` directly. Browsers do not let the app run that way. Start it with `start.bat` or `start.command`, and use the address the launcher prints, normally `http://localhost:4242`."],["h","The app window is empty, or the browser cannot reach the page"],["p","The local server is not running. This happens when you open the installed app or a bookmark without starting the launcher first, or after you closed the launcher window. Start the launcher again."],["h","The address is not localhost:4242"],["p","Something else on your computer was already using port 4242, so the server chose another one. That is fine. The launcher window prints the address it is using."],["h","The app starts twice"],["p","If the app is already running, starting the launcher again does not start a second copy. It opens a window on the one that is running."],["h","The app opened in a normal browser tab"],["p","The launcher found no Chrome, Edge or Brave, so it used your default browser. The app works the same in a tab."],["h","The window looks squeezed, or has scroll bars"],["p","The window cannot be smaller than 960 by 560 pixels. In a smaller window the app scrolls instead of squeezing its panels. Make the window bigger, or drag the edge of a sidebar to make the sidebar narrower. The left one cannot go under 220 pixels and the right one not under 240. See [The window](#the-window)."],["h","The 3D view is empty or looks wrong"],["list",["Press [[F]] to frame the model, and [[Alt + H]] to show hidden parts.","The viewer uses WebGPU when the browser has it and falls back to WebGL2 when it does not. To switch by hand, open {{Settings}}, {{Performance}} and set {{Renderer}} to WebGL2. The page reloads.","If a message says the GPU was lost, reload the page.","Update your browser and your graphics driver."]],["h","A STEP conversion takes very long"],["p","Large assemblies take time, and the reading of names, colours and the tree is the slowest part. The progress card keeps showing the converter’s latest message while it works. To make it faster, change the import settings:"],["list",["Leave {{SHUO overrides}}, {{Layer attributes}} and {{Validation properties (mass, area)}} off.","Choose a coarser {{Tessellation quality}}.","Set {{Drop parts smaller than (% of model)}} above 0 to skip tiny fasteners.","For a huge file where you only need the shape, choose geometry only under {{Colours & materials}}."]],["p","{{Cancel}} on the progress card stops a conversion that is running."],["h","“Conversion failed”"],["p","A STEP file with nothing solid in it gets a plain message that says so. For any other failure, press {{Copy log}} on the progress card and read the last lines. To see everything the converter prints, drag the STEP file onto `test-converter.bat` or `test-converter.command`. Include the log when you report the problem."],["h","The model has no colours or no parts tree"],["p","A STEP file may have been converted with geometry only. Open it again and choose the option that reads colours, names and hierarchy under {{Colours & materials}}. A GLB, FBX, OBJ or STL file can only show what the file itself contains."],["h","The view stutters on a heavy model"],["list",["Open {{Settings}}, {{Performance}}. Keep {{Skip tiny parts}} and {{Lower the resolution if it stutters}} on, and try {{Quality}} on Low.","Make the model lighter. See [A sensible order to work in](#workflow)."]],["h","Small parts disappear while I turn the view"],["p","That is {{Skip tiny parts}} at work. They come back the moment the view stops. Switch it off under {{Settings}}, {{Performance}} if you prefer."],["h","A recent file asks me to pick it again"],["p","The browser needs a fresh permission before the app may read that file. Open it once more with {{Open file…}}, or drop it on the window."],["h","The exported model is the wrong size or lies on its side"],["p","Export again with a different {{Unit scale}} or the other {{Up axis}}. A model that came from Cinema 4D or a glTF already stands Y up, so choose {{Keep as in the scene}} for it. See [Export](#export)."],["h","Cinema 4D or Maya cannot open an FBX I exported long ago"],["p","FBX files written by versions before 0.14.0 are refused by the FBX software in Cinema 4D and Maya. Export the file again with this version, or open it in Blender and save it again."],["h","An export fails on a very large model"],["p","Text formats such as OBJ can run out of memory. Use GLB or STL, which are binary."],["h","I closed the window and the app is still running"],["p","Closing the window does not stop the server. Close the launcher window too, or next time use {{Menu}}, then {{Quit app}}."],["see",["requirements","first-start","faq","self-test"]]]},{"slug":"faq","group":"help","title":"Frequently asked questions","summary":"Short answers: cost and licence, privacy, browsers, file sizes, whether Python is needed, and where to report a bug.","blocks":[["h","Is it free?"],["p","Yes. MeshOptimiser is free and open source."],["h","What is the licence?"],["p","MIT. In short, you may use, copy and change it, also for commercial work, as long as the licence text stays with it. It comes with no warranty."],["h","Does my file leave my computer?"],["p","No. The viewer and the converter both run on your machine. See [Privacy and offline use](#privacy-offline)."],["h","Do I need an internet connection?"],["p","Only for the first start, which downloads the Python add-ons. After that the app runs offline, and so does the help window ([[F1]]). A few rarely used converters are fetched the first time they are needed."],["h","Which browsers work?"],["p","A recent Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. The viewer uses WebGPU and falls back to WebGL2. For the app to open in a window of its own, the launcher needs Chrome, Edge or Brave. Installing it as an app is offered by Chrome and Edge."],["h","Which systems does it run on?"],["p","Windows and macOS."],["h","Can I use it without Python?"],["p","No. The app is served by a small Python program on your computer, and STEP files are converted in Python. On Windows, the launcher offers to install Python for you if it is missing."],["h","Which Python version?"],["p","3.10, 3.11 or 3.12. Version 3.13 is not supported yet."],["h","How big a file can it handle?"],["p","There is no fixed limit in these pages, because it depends on your computer’s memory and graphics card. The app is tested on assemblies of thousands of parts and millions of triangles. The README’s example is a 320 MB STEP file. The local server accepts STEP files up to 4 GB."],["h","Does it change my original file?"],["p","No. Nothing reaches a file until you save a scene or export, and both write a new file."],["h","Can it write STEP files?"],["p","No. It reads STEP and writes mesh formats: GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL and PLY, and a parts list as CSV."],["h","Where is the converted file?"],["p","In the folder `inbox` inside the app folder. To put a file where you want it, save a scene or export."],["h","Can I undo everything?"],["p","Every editing action can be undone with [[Ctrl + Z]]. By default the history goes back 200 steps, and {{Settings}}, {{General}} can make it 50 or 500. {{Revert to source…}} in the {{Menu}} reloads the file as it was. See [Undo and redo](#undo-redo)."],["h","Why do I have to start it with a launcher?"],["p","Because the converter is a Python program, and a web page cannot start one. The launcher starts a small local server that connects the two."],["h","How do I update?"],["p","Download the new zip and unpack it into a new folder. See [Stop, restart and update](#stop-restart-update)."],["h","Where do I find help inside the app?"],["p","Press [[F1]]. The help window holds these same articles, with a search. See [The help window](#help-window)."],["h","Where do I report a bug or ask for a feature?"],["p","At `https://github.com/xpayn3/MeshOptimiser/issues`. The app’s mark in the top bar has a link called {{Report an issue}}. Say what you did, what you expected and what happened, and add the version number and the log. {{Settings}}, {{About}}, {{Copy details}} copies the version number, your browser and your graphics card for you."],["see",["troubleshooting","privacy-offline","what-it-is"]]]},{"slug":"self-test","group":"help","title":"Self-test","summary":"For the curious: the app can test itself. One address runs a suite of checks inside the live app and reports the results.","blocks":[["p","You do not need this to use the app. It is useful when you suspect that something is broken on your computer, or before you report a bug."],["h","Run it"],["steps",["Start the app.","Open the address below in the browser. If your launcher printed a different port, use that number.","The app starts driving itself: it works the parts tree, the toolbar, the windows and the right-click menu the way a user would.","Results appear in a panel at the bottom right, and in the console."]],["code","http://localhost:4242/?selftest"],["h","What it checks"],["p","Undo and redo, exports, saving a scene and opening it again, and more. Nothing is downloaded to your computer: the exports are captured in memory."],["h","Run part of it"],["p","Add a word after `selftest=` to run only the tests whose name contains it. This address runs the tests about groups:"],["code","http://localhost:4242/?selftest=groups"],["h","The hole filler has its own test"],["p","It needs Node.js, a separate program for running JavaScript, and nothing else. In a terminal in the app folder:"],["code","node tests/holefill.test.mjs"],["p","It builds plates, pockets, counterbores, engraved letters, a boss, a washer and a hollow box, runs the hole filler on them, and checks that the result is closed, has the right volume, and is untouched where it should be."],["see",["troubleshooting","fill-holes","command-line"]]]}]};
